Meeting status: Confirmed; Hybrid
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=987&MId=16305', '2026-10-01', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '16305', '', '2026-10-01', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-07T07:54:11.953652', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('41a34880d2050a50147b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', 'In the first point, really, you were querying about the midpoint, and I think I''ve tried to explain. I think we said in our manifesto it was going to be around £400 million additional, so what we''re seeing as the midpoint—that was modelled on a midpoint as well. It’s the common practice. So, the midpoint that we''ve modelled here is £375 million. But as I said, obviously—because this is demand-led, this is a new thing—we''re hoping that all the discussion around childcare will make sure—. And the point you made around simplifying the process too—we know that many people don''t take up childcare, not only because it''s disjointed, but because it''s difficult, it''s complex and there are so many different eligibility thresholds, things you''ve got to apply for at different points during your child''s life. So, I can assure you that the point around simplification and what that looks like, what that front door looks like, is absolutely part of this work as well. And my officials are already working on that. So, it isn''t higher, actually; it''s around the same as what we projected, but we do recognise, of course, that there are all kinds of variables as we push forward towards the next phases that we need to allow for. But I do welcome your support for this initiative, for this policy, and, as I said, I agree absolutely on your point around simplifying the system.

 On your point around the way this policy interacts with other policy areas, it does, obviously, around education. Part of this work is about—. We''ve seen, haven''t we, it''s been exacerbated since COVID, but we''ve seen for a long time that families do need more support in order to make sure that their children are ready to learn, and this is going to play a big part in that. So, I''m having conversations—I actually met yesterday again with the Cabinet Minister for Education and the Welsh Language. We are working on how we can measure that, how we can evaluate the effect that this will have on that readiness to learn while keeping that absolutely child-centred, that evaluation. So, it will interact across Government and, as I said, especially with health, the interventions, the team around the family, and that support we will absolutely retain, but in a different form as we roll out this universal system.

 You asked me about exactly how the additional funding from the supplementary budget was going to be utilised. We''ve said that we''re going to invest an additional £55 million in the additional budget. So, hopefully, if you support this policy, you can support that budget this afternoon. Twenty million pounds of that will help to accelerate the roll-out, so that''s the work we''ve indicated to local authorities that they''re able to utilise in order to make sure that we get to the point where funded childcare is available to all two-year-olds in Wales by the end of this financial year. Then there''s £10 million of capital funding, which is going to help enhance and expand childcare settings, improving quality and capacity and accessibility of early years provision. And then £25 million of that is going to be used to support the wider system, including managing rising demand, because we''re talking about it a lot more now—it''s been such a front-and-centre policy during the election. People are coming to access more provision, which is great. We need to make it easier for them and more affordable for them. We want to strengthen the long-term sustainability of providers, and that''s something you talked about as well. And that is something that is absolutely crucial as well, and, again, is part of the work that we''re doing. So, we''re already investing in that, and, of course, supporting Welsh-medium childcare is part of that as well.

 So, on the last point on safeguarding, I think it is a really important point, and quality, you know, with the national minimum standard, which was updated last month. Wales has the highest national standards issued under the Children and Families (Wales) Measure 2010, and the Child Minding and Day Care (Wales) Regulations 2010, and Care Inspectorate Wales, of course is a regulatory body, so we will always make sure that anyone who is registered for providing day care and child minding will be inspected according to those regulations.

 We are looking at creative solutions. You know, I think we really need to consider child minders in this, who are constantly coming up as ''excellent'' and ''good'' when they''re inspected. They provide such a valuable service for people who work irregular hours, especially in places where there isn''t a density of population. So, we will absolutely be considering other models of provision as we roll this out.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=13204&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.462920', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767807', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('43e7a83cd44cf59b8b02', 'consultation', 'Senedd — committee consultation', 'Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 'Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee

This is an open Senedd consultation. Responding puts NRLA''s position formally on the record.

Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee, start date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 00:00:00 GMT, end date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 23:59:00 GMT

Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee Priorities Consultation: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee View the background to this consultation View all current consultations Purpose of the consultation The Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee has been set up by the Senedd to look at policy and legislation, and to hold the Welsh Government to account on specific issues. This includes equality and human rights, fair work, community cohesion and safety, tackling poverty, and social justice. In addition, the Committee may investigate any area of policy from the perspective of equality and human rights. During the autumn 2026, the Committee will be considering its strategic approach, objectives and ways of working. We would like to invite you to share your views on what you consider the Committee’s priorities for the Seventh Senedd should be. To submit your views, please complete the online form . The Committee are keen to hear from as many people and organisations as possible. If you would like to submit your views but do not want/are not able to complete the online form, please contact the Committee at SeneddEquality@Senedd.Wales and the Committee’s clerks can arrange with you a different way for your voice to be heard. The Committee’s postal address and telephone number can be found at the end of the consultation page. The closing date for submissions is Friday 18 September 2026 . Providing Written Evidence The Senedd has two official languages, Welsh and English. In line with the Senedd’s Official Languages Scheme , the Committee welcomes contributions in both or either of our official languages, and we ask organisations that are subject to Welsh Language standards or schemes to respond in line with their own obligations. Please inform the Committee when submitting responses if you intend to provide a translation at a later date. Please see guidance for those providing evidence for committees . Disclosure of information Please ensure that you have considered the Senedd’s policy on disclosure of information before submitting information to the Committee. Contact details Should you wish to speak to someone regarding this consultation, please use the below contact details: Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee Welsh Parliament Cardiff Bay Cardiff CF99 1SN Email: SeneddEquality@senedd.wales Telephone: 0300 200 6565 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign

Closing date: 18 September 2026.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgConsultationDisplay.aspx?ID=631', '2026-07-23', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd committee', '', '', '', '2026-09-18', 224.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'NRLA named', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; closing date; consultation; consultations; written evidence', 1, '2026-08-04T14:02:42.866340', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgRss.aspx?f=76', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('455c1685e0b1457375cf', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'As the former Chair of the Finance Committee, I reflect on what we were told by former finance Ministers about the fiscal position of the previous Government. I''ve also been following what the new finance Minister has had to say in her analysis of her inherited tight fiscal position, once the actual line-by-line detail has been seen. It also appears that things have got even tighter in recent months as a result of events out of this Government''s control. Nevertheless, it''s good to see that Plaid Cymru''s manifesto commitments are being delivered in this legislative programme this afternoon. I firmly believe that these commitments are for the benefit of the people and communities that we all represent—for example, the community right to buy.

 Not only that, these commitments are in line with the pledges of some of the opposition parties in this Siambr, as we''ve heard this afternoon. I''ve even agreed with one or two things that Darren Millar has said this afternoon, which is quite something. I hope, therefore, that we''re able to put aside some of the political posturing and point scoring in order to find the common ground. So, First Minister, will you agree with me that we should focus on the common ground and those outcomes that benefit people the length and breadth of Wales? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10277&l=en', NULL, 88.0, 'High', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; community right to buy; legislative programme; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.167489', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767766', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('46279214c93c92d11f6a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Item 8, legislative consent motion, Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill. I call on the Cabinet Minister for Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy to move the motion—Adam Price.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=20135&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.217391', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767871', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('47682430fbace53c5d37', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Reform believes Britain must do everything it can to retain the ability to make its own steel. Our MPs supported this Bill in Westminster, and today we will support it in the Senedd. But, we have to be very clear, this Bill comes far too late. The workers and their families at Port Talbot know that better than anyone. For years, they were promised certainty, instead they got delay. They were promised a plan and, despite the £1.25 billion transition programme, including up to £500 million of taxpayer support, many workers are still asking what replaces the secure skilled jobs that have been lost. Instead of those jobs, they got managed decline. Thanks to praying at the altar of net zero, we all now know that economic decline comes hand in hand with net zero. The blast furnaces have gone, thousands of livelihoods have been affected, and Britain has become more dependent on imported steel as a result.

 More than 2,100 Tata Steel jobs had been lost by September last year, with many of those losses concentrated in and around Port Talbot. Behind every one of those figures is a family, a household and a community that deserved better from its Government. While countries like India and China continue to expand their steel making, forging ahead using a balanced energy mix, what we have is not an industrial strategy that people can rely on, it''s failure on an industrial scale.

 These decisions haven''t affected climate change one bit, or even contributed to solving it. They''ve moved the problem elsewhere, if not, indeed, made it worse by having to ship product around the world and weakening our national resilience. The closure of Port Talbot''s blast furnaces have been linked to almost two thirds of the reduction in UK emissions between 2023 and 2025, yet global emissions do not disappear. When that production moves overseas, they are simply exported along with the jobs. The only achievement here is economic decay and destroying Welsh communities.

 Where intervention is needed to protect steel making, it should be decisive and focused on rebuilding a competitive industry. Our steel workers deserve an industry with a future. Our nation demands a strong steel industry and a Government with a credible strategy, one that protects jobs, strengthens Wales, and secures Britain''s industrial independence. This Bill is a necessary step towards that and it has our support. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Jason O''Connell', '', '12158', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=20843&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.298393', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767863', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('47e4c1b22b6bff68cd5d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'The fact that I''m now a Minister in Government doesn''t change my values at all. I continue to be a campaigner as well as a Minister in the Welsh Government, and I''m sure that other members of the Cabinet agree with that.

 There is a place for pension funds in Wales to invest responsibly and locally. Of course, although, as you note, we don''t have direct responsibility over pension funds, there are discussions that happen, and we do encourage pension authorities to invest responsibly and ethically, so that public sector pensions contribute positively to the future of Wales—economically, socially and environmentally.

 One excellent use of pension funds is investing in the social housing stock, and you know that I''m campaigning for more social housing. I understand that the city and county of Swansea pension fund has just created a fund worth £51 million that will invest in social housing on a local level. I''m very supportive of that step.

 The Wales Pension Partnership is responsible for explaining how they will invest. That doesn''t mean not investing in the defence industry, for example, but it does mean not investing in companies that produce controversial arms. So, already there is an ethical framework for the Wales Pension Partnership.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3101&l=en', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.982006', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768082', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('4f707fa90d0a3ee1f871', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Thank you. I now call on the Cabinet Minister to reply to the debate.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21743&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.399570', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767940', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
