Meeting status: Confirmed
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=983&MId=16248', '2026-09-30', '', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '', '16248', '', '2026-09-30', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-06T08:13:49.233425', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('5251059070816593821f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Diolch, Ddirprwy Lywydd. Cabinet Minister, thank you for your statement on Monday. I was hopeful that a statement like that would have come to this Chamber for a deeper debate, so I''ll use one of my questions on it. While the ambition to increase the supply of social housing is absolutely welcomed, your written statement confirms that acquired properties will count towards the 20,000 homes target, alongside new-build developments. Now, many people will hear this announcement and assume it means 20,000 newly built social homes. However, by including the acquisitions, the target appears to rely in part on existing properties already within Wales''s housing stock, rather than wholly on the creation of new homes. Cabinet Minister, can you tell us how many of the 20,000 additional social homes you expect to be delivered through acquisitions and how many will be genuinely new-build properties?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4490&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'new homes; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.123839', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768171', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('525a4a71cb92a08ec3c4', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'I recognise the platform that the Member has to lay out a number of political viewpoints, on much of which legislation is not needed. I could refer to legislation that he did propose that I just disagree on—to bring back the damage caused by Margaret Thatcher on right to buy, the damage that we are still trying to undo in terms of delivering more social housing now. So, we just disagree, okay; we''re just going to have to agree to disagree on that point.

 On the mobile phone ban, we''re taking action. Statutory guidance means we can move quicker without waiting for legislation. Though this Government has said we will legislate if we need to, we reckon we can work quicker than that.

 On tax maintenance, he somehow rubbishes the idea of tax maintenance. This is an important part of a well-functioning tax system, and, for a party that pride themselves on prudence, I''m surprised at the Member for taking such a cavalier attitude to making sure that we have good and effective taxation systems.

 On business rates, again, we don''t need to wait to act. Why doesn''t the Government legislate to bring back disused properties into use? Because we don''t need to legislate on that, necessarily. There''s action that is being taken right now on that.

 On fair funding, and making sure that we have a formula, not legislation, that ensures that money is shared out in an equitable way, well, those meetings are already meetings that my Minister for local government is having.

 Finally, there''s a theme from the Member, and I will allow him to follow whichever theme he wants, but the idea that this is a Government that hasn''t, in everything that we''ve done, made clear our determination to be consensual, from the very first comments that I made here in this Siambr—. I will read out again, Llywydd, my concluding statement in this statement this afternoon. I said that this is a programme that reflects the core values of this Government, and one that I hope will lead to co-operation and the sharing of ideas further among everybody within this Chamber. We are determined to develop legislation on the grounds of building consensus and through co-operation in the interests of all communities in Wales. I''m sorry that he''d switched off at that point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10108&l=en', NULL, 115.5, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; social_housing', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Legislative commitment', 'business rates; right to buy; social housing; we will legislate', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.144917', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767779', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('5437c210dd3b5eafac3e', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28', 'Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28

Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 Consultation: Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings Related Issues The Welsh Government must lay its draft budget for the next financial year before the Senedd to allow scrutiny of its proposals by the Finance Committee . Other Senedd Committees may also consider the draft budget before it is finalised. The Welsh Government has announced that it is planning to publish its Draft Budget on 17 November 2026, and the Final Budget on 16 February 2027. ¬¬¬ Finance Committee Correspondence >>>> >>> Letter to the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Budget Timetable 2027-28- 10 July 2026 (PDF 114KB). >>> Letter to the Llywydd and Chair of Business Committee: Budget Timetable 2027-28 - 10 July 2026 (PDF 115KB). >>> Letter from the Llywydd and Chair Of the Business Committee: Budget Timetable 2027-28- 7 July 2026 (PDF 223KB). >>> Letter from the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Budget Timetable 2027-28 -3 July 2026 (PDF 188KB). >>> >>> >>> >>> >>> >>> >>> >>> <<< zzz Business type: Budget Scrutiny Reason considered: Senedd Business; Status: For consideration First published: 13/07/2026 Documents Hide the documents Letter to the Llywydd and Chair of Business Committee: Welsh Government Draft Budget Timetable 2027-28 - 10 July 2026 PDF 115 KB 2026 07 10 Chair to Minister re timetable-e PDF 114 KB Consultations Welsh Government Draft Budget 2027-28 (running until 25/09/2026) Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47822', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 147.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Consultation open', 'Finance Committee; consultation; consultations; draft budget; final budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:49.357937', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47822', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('54407e8a78f965a5378e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', 'I thank the Member for raising this important issue. Clearly, in terms of empowering communities, that''s something that this Government has committed to. We certainly believe that we need to look again at processes in terms of planning and community engagement in order to ensure that the voice of local residents and local people is heard. It was one of the reasons why a number of us campaigned when we were in the opposition parties in terms of a number of the measures that are in our manifesto and our programme for government. The voice of communities is important and it''s very difficult when they don''t have the kind of resources that they need to proceed with the kinds of studies that you mentioned when companies, sometimes international ones, have so much money that can support them to challenge things against the community.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=8210&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.929167', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767717', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('54f492616f35a55391b3', 'research', 'Senedd Research', 'A “first phase of legislation”: the Welsh Government’s legislative priorities', 'A “first phase of legislation”: the Welsh Government’s legislative priorities

Published 28/07/2026 | Reading Time minutes

On 14 July 2026, the First Minister, Rhun ap Iorwerth MS, set out the “first phase of legislation” that the Welsh Government will introduce to the Senedd. This covers areas including the right to buy community assets, renting protections and water regulation.

This article looks at these legislative proposals in more detail and how opposition parties have responded.

The First Minister committed to develop a community right-to-buy scheme, and provide the “legislative basis to enable eligible community groups to nominate and register valuable community assets to be given the right of first refusal when the owners decide to sell”.

The previous Welsh Government established a Community Asset Commission, which recommended that the Welsh Government should “introduce Community Right to Buy legislation in Wales that will be applicable across public, private and third sectors”.

Proposals relating to community right to buy were introduced in England recently.

The First Minister also committed to strengthen the rights of tenants in the private rented sector. The Welsh Government will introduce legislation that aims to strengthen the enforcement framework and regulatory role of Rent Smart Wales .

These changes are intended to “lay the groundwork” for further changes to improve housing affordability, make rents fairer, and limit no-fault evictions, which will be brought forward later in the Senedd term.

The First Minister also said the Welsh Government is developing proposals to legislate for the “right to adequate housing”.

Senedd Research has previously considered the differences between tenancy legislation in Wales and England

The First Minister committed to proposals that “support the developing climate and nature action plan”, by making existing environmental legislation “clearer, more workable and better able to support delivery” in a range of areas. The new Climate and Nature Plan will aim to deliver net zero by 2040 and substantive nature recovery by 2050.

Senedd Research recently considered what’s next for nature and climate action.

The First Minister committed to developing a “statutory duty on rural-proofing”, to “ensure rural needs are considered consistently when policies are developed and delivered”.

A consultation on this and the above environmental proposals was launched on 22 July 2026. It is yet to be decided whether the proposals on rural proofing and environmental sustainability will be taken forward through the same or separate Bills.', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/a-first-phase-of-legislation-the-welsh-government-s-legislative-priorities/', '2026-07-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 335.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; rent_controls_and_affordability; evictions_and_possession; rent_smart_wales; homelessness; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Legislative commitment; Consultation open', 'Rent Smart Wales; adequate housing; community asset; community assets; community right to buy; consultation; evictions; housing affordability; introduce legislation; legislative priorities; no-fault evictions; private rented; private rented sector; right to adequate housing; right to buy; tenancy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:45.480643', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('56a582d18c2d5099a92a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'As I''ve already spoken on in this Chamber, councils are in real financial distress. We are seeing massive increases in council tax, growing debt bubbles and serious service cuts. This is why the Government''s plan to review the funding formula is so important. However, as with everything, Minister, detail is key. Strong words and bold promises aren''t enough. So, with local government on tenterhooks waiting for this review, can you tell us how you intend to review the formula? What components are you looking to change and, most importantly, how will the Welsh Government define success when it''s reviewing the formula? What does a successful review look like to the Welsh Government? Minister, this place and, most importantly, local government need answers.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Tom Montgomery', '', '12183', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=6085&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'council tax', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.748244', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767661', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('57e17a25de8c9088bdef', 'oral_question', 'Question not reached in Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '<p>The new changes to the energy efficiency requirements of the building regulations in Wales will come into force in March 2027. These will mandate requirements for low-carbon heating systems, such as heat pumps or heat networks, and renewable electricity generation, such as solar panels, in all newly built dwellings.</p>', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', '', 'Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '', '', '', 'Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 65.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'heat pumps', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:06.994042', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=QNR', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('582d167e829798668c56', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'SL(7)002 - The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026', 'SL(7)002 - The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026

SL(7)002 - The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026 SL(7)002 - The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026 Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings Related Issues Subject to the Senedd approval procedure Date Laid: 23 June 2026 Coming into force: Coming into force in accordance with regulation ​ 1(2) Date of Committee meeting: 13 July 2026 Report Status: Technical, Merits Date of Plenary Meeting: Outcome: On 14 July 2026, the Business Committee agreed that the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee should report to the Senedd on the draft Regulations by 25 September 2026. On 21 July 2026, The Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee published a report outlining its approach to scrutiny of the Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026. On 24 July 2026, the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee wrote to stakeholders seeking their views on The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026. Business type: Subordinate Legislation Status: For consideration First published: 23/06/2026 Documents SL(7)002 - The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026 EM SL(7)002 - The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026 Welsh Government response, 3 July 2026 PDF 6 KB View as HTML (3) 4 KB Welsh Government response, 10 July 2026 Letter from the Chair of the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee to Stakeholders regarding The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026 – 24 July 20266 (e) PDF 173 KB Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47724', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 140.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Legislative commitment; Bill progress', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; coming into force; draft regulations; subordinate legislation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:53.267501', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47724', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('5898b6f3009df5b8b622', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '6. What progress has the Welsh Government made towards establishing a tenure-neutral retrofitting programme? OQ64397', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Safa Elhassan', '', '12189', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'retrofitting', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.251700', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768196', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('58b23d4c2a29691baf60', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'The ALN budget ask was brought to the negotiating table by Labour. I accept and respect that, Lynne. That''s what a three-week scrutiny process allows to happen. Between a draft budget and the final vote on the budget, it can be improved. And we have moved and engaged on the detail of the proposal with you. And our education Minister was, as you know, keen to have your support to commence this additional budget for ALN as soon as possible. Schools would have returned in September with this new ALN funding ready to be rolled out—£40 million this year, followed by at least another £80 million over the next two years.

 Defeating this supplementary budget today will mean that this Government will continue without an early supplementary budget. We will carry on to spend in line with the annual budget passed in January. We will be able to continue to roll out the work on NHS waiting lists, increasing free childcare, free school meals and swimming lessons and £1 bus fares, and to boost capital spend on schools, hospitals, social housing and community facilities, all within the spending profiles already passed by this Senedd in the budget in January. But we will not be able to do that and add an in-year additional allocation of £40 million for additional learning needs, because we will not have an assurance of a supplementary budget. That''s not spite, Lynne—that is prudent budget management within this financial year.

 But this Government is keen to improve on the previous Government''s allocation to ALN and to work with schools and councils on a long-term stabilisation plan for ALN. We will now move on to this work in the context of our first annual budget. Somehow—[ Interruption .] Ah, you''re timing me now. You didn''t time me in the first contribution, so I have no idea how much time I took.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26187&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'draft budget; social housing; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.826989', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767997', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('58d10ce35ed0e38b8e5d', 'calendar', 'Senedd forward look', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee — 30 September 2026, 09.00', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee is scheduled to meet on 30 September 2026 at 09.00.
Meeting status: Confirmed
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=978&MId=16234', '2026-09-30', '', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '', '16234', '', '2026-09-30', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-06T08:13:49.236775', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('58de830b3a58e17a5806', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'This question reminds me, in a slightly odd way, of the days when many of us were fighting to convince our pension schemes—in my case the Universities Superannuation Scheme—to divest from fossil fuels, landmines, cluster bombs and tobacco. It''s quite the converse of that fight. From time to time, a public sector pension executive would sidle up to me and quietly remind me that the best return on investment could be achieved from the lucrative international tobacco market, selling cigarettes to children in countries with less regulation than we have here in Wales. We pushed our pension schemes to invest positively in things like renewable energy and social housing, and the returns were good. Does the Minister agree with me that making positive, sustainable choices for our pensions is not only the moral thing to do, but it yields great, long-term returns for our pensions, returns that do not depend on global instability or risk stranded fossil fuel assets?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Paul Rock', '', '12176', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3328&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister; LGHP Committee members', '', 'social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.000683', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768117', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('594938d4f5facce7e3b2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I rise today to address yet another example of Welsh Government introducing policy without fully understanding the consequences. I refer to the 182-day occupancy requirement for self-catering accommodation providers. The policy was supposed to help tackle housing pressures and the impact of second homes. Instead, it is placing genuine tourism businesses under increasing strain, particularly in Ceredigion Penfro. 

 Since 2023, holiday-let operators have had to achieve 182 days of commercial occupancy to qualify for business rates rather than council tax. If they fail to meet the threshold, they can face significant additional costs, often including second home premiums. In theory, Ministers may argue that this sounds reasonable, but governing is not done in theory. It must be rooted in the realities faced by communities in Tenby, Saundersfoot, Newport, St Davids, Aberaeron, Newquay and countless other coastal and rural towns whose economies depend heavily upon seasonal tourism. Owners cannot control the weather. They cannot control rising living costs that reduce holiday bookings. They cannot control wider economy uncertainty, and they also cannot control the fact that west Wales remains a highly seasonable tourism market. Yet Welsh operators face one of the highest occupancy thresholds anywhere in these islands.

 Reports suggest that almost 40 per cent of operators have failed to meet the target while many are now questioning whether their business should remain viable. These are not large investors. They are family-run businesses that support jobs and local economies. They employ local cleaners, gardeners, tradespeople and maintenance workers. They support cafes, pubs, restaurants, shops and visitor attractions across our communities.

 What is particularly difficult to justify is the comparison with England. There is the equivalent threshold of 70 nights. In Wales, it is 182—more than double. So, where is the evidence that this higher threshold is delivering better outcomes? Has housing become more affordable in Pembrokeshire and Ceredigion? Has the housing supply significantly increased? Or has this policy simply created financial pressures and uncertainty for local tourism and businesses? The evidence increasingly points to the latter. 

 The Government''s consultation generated over 1,200 responses, yet the result was largely administrative changes and technical exemptions. The central issue remained untouched: the threshold itself. This is not about protecting second homes, or those who exploit loopholes, it is about recognising that legitimate tourism businesses should not be penalised because they cannot guarantee year-round demand. The realities of Ceredigion and Penfro are not the realities of Cardiff. A one-size-fits-all approach simply does not work.

 At Reform UK, we believe that Government should support enterprise, not burden it. If housing supply is the problem, build more homes. If affordability is the issue, reform planning and increase housing delivery. But please do not place the blame on small businesses that create jobs and support one of Wales''s most important industries. So, I urge this Government to think again. Please review the 180-day rule, work with the tourism sector and local communities and introduce a fairer, more flexible approach that reflects the realities of rural Wales. Because good policy should be based on evidence, common sense and economic reality, not arbitrary targets that risk damaging the very communities that they claim to help. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Paul Marr', '', '12180', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=13656&l=en', NULL, 148.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; housing_supply', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open', '182 days; business rates; consultation; council tax; housing delivery; housing supply; second home; second homes; self-catering', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.934431', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767084', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('5e420bda48f0fc40376e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I''m going to echo some of the things we''ve heard already today, but with a slightly different take. So, first off, tourism is one of Wales''s greatest success stories. It does support around 159,000 jobs—almost one in every eight in Wales. Millions of visitors come here because of our coastline, countryside, history and culture. They support jobs, sustain businesses and bring vital income to many of our rural and coastal communities. Nowhere is that more evident than in my constituency of Gŵyr Abertawe, where the visitor economy contributes more than £700 million annually and supports around 5,500 jobs.

 For many communities, tourism is not simply an important industry, it is the industry. It supports family businesses, keeps our high streets alive and helps sustain the very communities that people want to visit. When tourism prospers, our community prospers. But that prosperity depends on businesses having the confidence to invest, employ local people and plan for the future. Tourism businesses understand the need to support local communities and recognise the pressures on the housing market; what they need are policies that reflect the commercial realities of a seasonal industry.

 We all share the same ambition: a successful tourism industry alongside communities where local people can afford to live. Indeed, your own manifesto recognised the importance of tourism, and I quote:

 ''Plaid Cymru recognises the importance of the tourism industry to Wales.''

 ''We will promote tourism which provides maximum benefit to our people, environment, language and culture''.

 I agree with that ambition. The question is not the destination, the question is whether the route chosen is actually taking us there.

 The issue I want to focus on today is the 182-day occupancy requirement for self-catering holiday accommodation. Let me be clear: this debate is not about whether there should be a qualifying threshold—the tourism industry accepts that there should be one; the question is whether 182 days is realistic, proportionate and evidence based.

 Representatives from across the tourism industry have consistently argued that 105 days is a more realistic threshold for demonstrating genuine commercial activity. His Majesty''s Revenue and Customs recognise 105 days of actual letting as a qualifying test for the furnished holiday lettings tax regime. Ministers may argue that the purposes are different, but the principle remains the same: identifying genuine commercial activity. And, I have to say, persuading HMRC to agree to anything outside their own way of thinking is no small achievement. It''s certainly not an organisation renowned for changing its mind simply because somebody asked politely. So, what evidence led the previous Welsh Government, with Plaid Cymru support, to conclude that 182 days was a necessary and proportionate figure? Why 182? What modelling or economic assessment supported that decision? If that evidence exists, publish it.

 The Government''s stated objective was to increase the supply of homes for local people, but intentions do not automatically create good outcomes. The assumption behind the policy was that raising the threshold would return more properties to the residential market. But has that happened to any meaningful extent? Many tourism businesses report that income has fallen off significantly, with some parts of the self-catering sector reporting reductions of around 30 per cent. The Welsh Government''s own tourism business barometer highlights continuing pressures within the sector. But reducing holiday lets was never the objective. The real test is whether more homes have become available for local people. So, the question remains: how many properties have actually returned to the residential housing market because of this policy? How many homes have become available to local families? What measurable improvement has there been in housing affordability or availability?

 If the Government believes this policy has succeeded, publish the evidence. I understand Ministers are considering further flexibility and additional exemptions, and I welcome the fact that they are listening. But there is a certain irony here. A policy introduced to solve one problem is now requiring exemptions to solve the problems created by the policy itself. The tourism industry is not asking for special interest. The message is loud and clear: it is asking for a system that is fair, proportionate and based on evidence. So, Cabinet Minister, if you remain convinced that 182 days is the correct figure, publish the evidence that justifies choosing it. Publish the evidence that shows it has delivered more homes for local people. Because if this policy requires an ever-growing list of exemptions simply to make it workable, perhaps the problem is not the exemptions, perhaps it is the policy itself. Exemptions are not the answer. Good government is measured by whether policies deliver the outcomes they promise. If the evidence shows that this one is not, then we need real and substantial change. I call on all Members to support our motion. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Steven Rodaway', '', '12191', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12421&l=en', NULL, 93.5, 'High', 'digest', 'rent_controls_and_affordability; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Private rented sector', '', '', '182 days; holiday lets; housing affordability; self-catering', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.790908', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767079', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('5fa44d18bdbf3aa157a3', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much. That takes us on very neatly from that previous response, because progress is being made, and, certainly, in our manifesto, you will recall that establishing a tenure-neutral retrofit programme was a crucial part of that. So, I hope that that will lead to a supplementary question—I forget that there''s a supplementary question.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5779&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'retrofit', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.253257', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768197', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('60c977462cb51acc8958', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'Thank you very much for those comments and questions, and, yes, it''s good to be able to stand here and make a statement that we are prioritising these areas. We''ve been talking about them for many years and now we can make progress. What we have here, essentially, is a programme just for the first 18 months, perhaps. So, the community right to buy is something that will happen within that period so that we can demonstrate that we are moving quickly. There are elements, as I''ve mentioned, in terms of the first part of legislation to protect the rights of renters, which again will happen in this first phase. But we then need to develop within the rest of the time of this Government the further legislation that can actually move on to protect the right to an adequate home and further protection for renters. So, there is a developing programme here. Bear in mind too, and I would again refer to the cross-Senedd co-operation that I hope to see, there''ll be an opportunity for new legislation to be introduced into this programme. Parliamentary committees may propose legislation. Individuals on the backbenches will have opportunities from time to time. So, there is a dynamic programme here, but the important thing, and I''m pleased to hear support from the Member on this, is that we are starting to address these issues that we know mean so much to the people that we represent.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10611&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community right to buy; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.209499', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767782', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('63ae6fcc5d76b9f2813f', 'consultation', 'Welsh Government — Consultation (notification)', 'Consultation: Council Tax Reduction Scheme in Wales - technical consultation on reassessment thresholds', 'Consultation: Council Tax Reduction Scheme in Wales - technical consultation on reassessment thresholds
We want your views on the changes we want to make to the Council Tax Reduction Scheme in Wales.

 The technical consultation covers reassessment thresholds and how changes in a household''s circumstances trigger a reassessment of entitlement.

 Read the consultation: https://www.gov.wales/council-tax-reduction-scheme-wales-technical-consultation-reassessment-thresholds', 'https://www.gov.wales/council-tax-reduction-scheme-wales-technical-consultation-reassessment-thresholds', '2026-07-15', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', '', '', '', NULL, 105.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; council tax; council tax reduction scheme; we want your views', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:54.643225', 'mailbox:senedd-monitor@nrla.org.uk:FIXTURE-002', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6477f86f415dbc966e96', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'On legislation, I always think about how comprehensive education was initially brought in following a Department for Education and Science circular by Anthony Crosland—no legislation. 

 I''m a member of the Co-operative Party, who have long campaigned for a community right-to-buy scheme, and I support legislation to enable eligible community groups to nominate and register valuable community assets and be given the right to first refusal when the owner decides to sell. Far too many community buildings are left to deteriorate, and community action could stop some of these becoming derelict. Is any funding going to be made available to help these community purchases?

 I also want us to leave a greener legacy for generations to come, and I welcome the environmental proposal to support developing a climate and nature action plan. What I will ask, though, on animals, is: will the proposed legislation provide additional support for animals in Wales? And again I ask for legislation to stop the private ownership of primates.

 I note in the First Minister''s statement that control over water runs far deeper in Wales''s collective memory than it being seen merely as a matter of policy or regulation. Your economy Minister joined me in the last Senedd in supporting the public ownership of water. Will the Welsh Government consider nationalising Welsh Water?

 Finally, on the economy, will the Welsh Government consider funding university research in computing and life sciences and the spin-offs that come from it?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Mike Hedges', '', '332', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10709&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'community assets', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.217563', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767788', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('649c5baa015daa10782e', 'consultation', 'Senedd — committee consultation', 'Priorities for the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 'Priorities for the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee

This is an open Senedd consultation. Responding puts NRLA''s position formally on the record.

Priorities for the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee, start date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 00:00:00 GMT, end date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:59:00 GMT

Priorities for the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee Priorities for the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee View the background to this consultation View all current consultations Purpose of the consultation Purpose of the consultation The Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee (‘the Committee’) is holding an inquiry to inform its priorities for the Seventh Senedd. Terms of reference: To help determine its priorities, the Committee is seeking views on: >>>> >>> What do you consider to be the main priorities or issues that the Committee should consider during the Seventh Senedd, and why? >>> Rank your top three priorities. Please rank the issues you have identified above in order of priority, with 1 being the highest priority. >>> Are there any other comments you would like to make about the Committee''s priorities, its future work programme, or how it carries out its work? <<<< Submitting your views The Committee would like to invite you to submit written evidence to assist in its inquiry by completing this online form . We are keen to hear from as many people and organisations as possible. If you would like to submit your views but do not want/are not able to complete the online form, please write to the Committee (SeneddHinsawdd@Senedd.Cymru) and the Committee’s clerks can arrange with you a different way for your voice to be heard. The Committee’s postal address and telephone number can be found at the end of the consultation page. The closing date for submission to this consultation is 11 September 2026 . Providing Written Evidence The Senedd has two official languages, Welsh and English. In line with the Senedd’s Official Languages Scheme , the Committee welcomes contributions in both or either of our official languages, and we ask organisations that are subject to Welsh Language standards or schemes to respond in line with their own obligations. Please inform the Committee when submitting responses if you intend to provide a translation at a later date. Please see guidance for those providing evidence for committees . Disclosure of information Please ensure that you have considered the Senedd’s policy on disclosure of information before submitting information to the Committee. Contact details Should you wish to speak to someone regarding this consultation, please use the below contact details: Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural

Closing date: 11 September 2026.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgConsultationDisplay.aspx?ID=623', '2026-07-21', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd committee', '', '', '', '2026-09-11', 224.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'NRLA named', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; closing date; consultation; consultations; inquiry; seeking views; terms of reference; written evidence', 1, '2026-08-04T14:02:41.969519', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgRss.aspx?f=76', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
