This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=987&MId=16256', '2026-09-17', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '16256', '', '2026-09-17', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:40.413523', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6f94c945c66e91343aa8', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Diolch, Minister. Following on from what our Reform spokesperson has already said, I would like to urge you further to have annual targets so that any progress on delivering those 20,000 social homes can be easily tracked. We all know that Rome wasn''t built in a day, but we would like to know how many homes will be built in Wales in a year.

 Minister, following alongside those aims to get people into social housing as soon as possible, I was wondering whether the pots of money currently given to registered social landlords to adapt old stock—current old stock—to meet the demand and needs of those on current waiting lists will continue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5635&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'registered social landlords; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.233884', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768220', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('708b06b6332f1f25b072', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Thank you, Cabinet Minister. The proposal is to agree the motion. Does any Member object? [ Objection .] There is objection. I will therefore defer voting under this item until voting time.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22026&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.435017', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767943', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('712bb047cc171409d8d8', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 'Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee

Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Consultation: Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. The Committee’s forward work programme for the autumn term is now available. Please note that the programme is subject to change and will be reviewed and updated regularly to reflect the Committee’s ongoing work. Forward work programme: Autumn 2026 (PDF 80KB) ¬¬¬ Consultation: Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee On the 9th July 2026 the Committee held its first meeting . During the meeting Members discussed options for determining a strategic approach to the work within their remit, including how to set out priority areas for scrutiny over the term. To help inform these discussions and to shape the Committee’s forward work programme, Members agreed to launch a consultation on priorities for the Seventh Senedd. The Committee is seeking views from people and organisations on the issues they believe it should prioritise in the Seventh Senedd. Further information on how to submit your views is available on the consultation page . Please share this consultation with anyone who you think would be interested in submitting their views. zzz Business type: Other First published: 11/06/2026 Documents Hide the documents Forward work programme: Autumn 2026 PDF 304 KB View as HTML (2) 54 KB Consultations Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee (running until 14/09/2026) Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47562', '2026-08-05', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 98.0, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; consultation; consultations; seeking views', 0, '2026-08-05T14:00:51.561947', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47562', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('714eecc5d7797af7d47b', 'committee_transcript', 'Petitions Committee', '2. Updates - Petitions referred to the Seventh Senedd', 'Option 2 is to refer the petition to the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee for scrutiny. If the petition is referred to another committee without any further correspondence with the Minister by the Petitions Committee, then this committee could then close its consideration of the petition. That wouldn''t be my choice. I don''t know how other people feel on this issue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16175', '2026-07-09', 'Donna Cushing', '', '12168', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. Updates - Petitions referred to the Seventh Senedd', '16175', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16175?startPos=401&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:13.216697', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16175&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767220', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('718af16707b144fd4b09', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', 'Since the dawn of devolution 27 years ago, the young people of Wales have been let down, both in terms of their education received and their attempts to find good employment, and the two are inextricably linked. It''s unforgiveable that a nation like ours with so much potential has become such an unattractive prospect for young people seeking employment, and that we are haemorrhaging precious home-grown talent in our youngsters and they are leaving Wales. But it''s little wonder. Wages here are lower and people pay a greater proportion of their income in tax than anywhere else in Britain. So, why would a young person, fresh out of education, yet to put down roots, not seek opportunities and settle elsewhere?

 The same stands for our businesses. Our young people—some of our young people—are the innovators of the future, the ones leaving education with fresh ideas for business start-ups. But with sky-high business rates and a poor infrastructure, transport wise, naturally, any business-savvy young person will look beyond Wales to minimise their costs and maximise their earnings.

 But even before our young people look at the job market, their choices have been limited by the worst performing education system in the UK. And I blame Welsh Labour and Plaid Cymru. They have far fewer opportunities than their counterparts over the border for courses, apprenticeships and degrees. Our infrastructure and transport networks play their part in this problem. They are suffering from a chronic lack of investment, and virtue-signalling policies like 20 mph and a ban on road building have damaged us and meant that business and jobs are not coming to Wales in anywhere near the numbers they should.

 I will say though that I very much welcome the commitment from the Welsh Government to a third Menai crossing in my own constituency of Bangor Conwy Môn, something that we have campaigned for for a long time. This will be critical to the expansion and new job opportunities at Wylfa, and that is exactly what we need to be replicating across Wales: investment in our transport infrastructure, building a much needed M4 relief road.

 Other harmful policies like the tourism tax. It just beggars belief why you keep hitting the very businesses, as Tom Montgomery actually put across quite well, that youngsters want to go into, in the hospitality industry. They want to work in a fish and chip shop or serve slushies at Woolworths. But we''re just doing everything we can to make it difficult for businesses to actually then encourage young people to go and work there.

 During the election, the Welsh Conservatives had a robust plan for economic growth. It really was the only credible plan to deliver more jobs and prosperity, and, critically, to bring us in line with the rest of the UK. Now, we are happy to work with Plaid Cymru, and I had a very constructive meeting with the Minister yesterday, Adam Price. We need to work together, as has been said, cross-party, but we need to be serious about using the levers that you have as a Welsh Government. You need to scrap business rates for small firms, pubs and post offices. We need to make public procurement contracts more accessible, particularly to our small business. We''ve been talking about this for 15 years, but it''s not happened. We must give priority to Welsh local firms.

 We need to work with education providers to expand the choice of courses available to young people, particularly in STEM subjects and apprenticeships. We need to take action to address mobile blackspots and poor broadband speeds, so that our young people in remote and rural communities aren''t left behind and their studies aren''t hindered. Crucially, we need to look at the problem with young people and unemployment in the round, starting with the improving of our education system, finishing with fixing our economy. That is the only way to create secure, well-paid jobs for our young people, to rival those of other UK nations and indeed the rest of the world.

 So, I agree with many of the really good points that have been made in this debate, but let''s not forget who is responsible for the mess we''re in with our job market. For Plaid, they''ve said all the right things about young people and employment before the election, but as I said on the day of my re-election, I will be really watching on this, because you will not grow Wales without growing your young people. I don''t feel very optimistic that you will deliver all you have promised, and we will hold you to account on it—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Janet Finch-Saunders', '', '212', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=10376&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'business rates; tourism tax', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.709663', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768285', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('73a9efa87573ad48190d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Yes, absolutely. Will you commit to amending the allocation of housing and homelessness regulations, to restrict the eligibility and give preference to British applicants? And will you use your legal powers to set a standard compelling every registered social landlord in Wales to record and publish the nationality of applicants and tenants?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4183&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'homelessness; registered social landlord', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.090431', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768181', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('74022678fdc26fc70b24', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Wales needs more houses. You''ve said that today, Minister, but we heard in the press over the weekend developers and housing associations saying that it can take between three and five years to secure planning consent.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5144&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'housing associations; planning consent', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.186351', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768174', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('76fc5e4371a029c8ace1', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Delivering meaningful change for people in Pen-y-bont Bro Morgannwg, and across Wales, is at the heart of this Government''s approach. That starts with our first 100 days plan, on which I''m working collaboratively with Cabinet colleagues to deliver. The programme for government will build on this foundation, with improved cross-Government working and a clear focus on delivery.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=4705&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.620795', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767664', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7908c036acae24a4b8fd', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Minister, this is a minority Government and, over the last few weeks, this Senedd has demonstrated that Plaid cannot simply assume that others will just fall into line and vote with them. If the Government wants to secure the support of this Chamber, it must be prepared to build the consensus across political divides and to listen to those who represent different viewpoints. Every one of us in this Chamber has been elected to represent our constituents and to honour the mandate they have given us. Our responsibility is not to make life easier for the Government, but to ensure that the voices of the people we represent are heard in this place.

 In Reform, our position has been clear from the outset. We have three simple red lines to ensure that we support a budget that actually puts Wales first. I''d like to first say that none of those red lines is directly copying what England are doing with the ALN funding, so I look forward to her apology for the comments she initially made regarding Reform''s position.

 The first red line is we called for international spending to be brought to an end, so that those millions of pounds could instead be invested here in Wales, as they always should have been. The Welsh Government budget was always intended to be spent in Wales.

 Secondly, we called for greater investment in our home-grown NHS staff. It is unacceptable that newly qualified Welsh nurses are completing their training only to find there are too few opportunities for them to work in the NHS here in Wales. We invested in training these professionals, yet this year, nearly 400 nurses and midwives could not find jobs in the NHS after initial job allocations. Coincidentally, the Welsh Government has hired at least 500 nurses and other medical professionals from India through the Wales in India initiative.

 Thirdly, we highlighted, as other parties have, the urgent need for greater support for the additional learning needs system. There is no excuse for not being aware of this strain, even before we all highlighted it to you. Concerns have been raised about these financial pressures from Audit Wales to local councils to school leaders.

 Unfortunately, on the first two priorities, the Government has failed to act. On additional learning needs, while the Government has acknowledged the issue, what has been offered to local councils is only a fraction of what is required for our schools. It falls well short of the level of support that school leaders have been calling for. It is clear that rather than attempting to fix the system, this is an offer designed to secure political support, treating disabled children like a political football, and I know my colleague will touch on this later.

 Llywydd, at the election, Reform became the largest opposition group in the history of the Senedd. That was not an accident. It reflected the desire of many people across Wales for a different approach and for different voices to be heard in Welsh politics. We were not elected to wave through Government budgets that, in our view, fail to deliver for the people of Wales. We were elected to hold the Government to account and to argue for policies that reflect the mandate we have received. The people who sent us here expect us to stand by our principles, and that is exactly what we''re doing today. The majority of people in Wales did not vote for Plaid''s manifesto, and the majority of the Members of the Senedd were not elected on its programme. The proportional representation system that Plaid helped to design was always going to result in a minority Government. You knew you needed to seek collaboration.

 From the very first sitting in this Senedd, Reform has made it clear that we are prepared to work constructively with Members from across the political spectrum where proposals genuinely benefit the people of Wales. That offer still remains on the table, but that offer requires collaboration, not the expectation that an opposition party will sheepishly support a budget that fails to deliver for their voters. And for these reasons, Llywydd, Reform will be voting against the supplementary budget.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Cai Parry-Jones', '', '12174', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23691&l=en', NULL, 33.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Delivery bodies', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.554714', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767966', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
