Forward work programme – Finance Committee Forward work programme – Finance Committee Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Finance Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. Business type: Other First published: 15/06/2026 Documents Forward Work Programme : Autumn 2026 PDF 59 KB Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47639', '2026-08-06', '', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 42.0, 'Low', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Finance Committee', 0, '2026-08-06T09:13:26.404924', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47639', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('aa6de4a6002bd0cf4d1f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Sera Evans, as Chair of the Early Years, Children, Young People and Education Committee. Sera.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=25385&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.744603', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767962', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ab5de7dbe25fd6fc9df3', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'The fact of the matter is it''s not something that we''re going to agree on. So, let me just finish off.

 First, on keeping the 182-day occupancy threshold for self-catering businesses under review, we absolutely support keeping this policy under review to ensure that it works as fairly and effectively as possible. Secondly, on the visitor levy, I''ve already set out the benefits. It''s common practice, it happens across many parts of the world, and Scotland are now also looking to follow suit. This is ultimately all about choice. It''s about giving choice and power to local people, to local councils, so that decisions that affect their communities can be made in those same communities. So, we will be supporting the Government amendment and we will continue to push for a thriving tourism economy all over Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12988&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', 'self-catering; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.868093', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767095', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ab660d30341a6bbd6e0c', 'legislation', 'Senedd Act', 'Planning (Wales) Act 2026', 'Planning (Wales) Act 2026
Current stage: Royal Assent (27 April 2026)
Stage history: Royal Assent 27 Apr 2026; Royal Assent 07 Apr 2026', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46434', '2026-04-27', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd', '', '', '', NULL, 117.0, 'High', 'digest', 'planning_system; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', 'Bill progress', 'Planning (Wales) Act; Royal Assent', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:20.261641', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46434', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ac9bc841f33152950463', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'It is supplementary to the budget that was presented and passed by the previous Labour Government. It was the deliberate decision of this Government to honour the spending commitments made by the previous Government, not to make changes to funding decisions made by Labour. In that context, I understand what the Conservatives have told me, that not changing the Labour budget makes it impossible for them to vote in favour of this supplementary budget today. I fully understand that.

 In addition, our decision too was to start to make some new spending commitments to align with the mandate that we received from the people of Wales through a national election. This budget, therefore, allocates £294 million, which is £164 million in resources and £130 million in capital, to support practical priorities that people can see and feel, as was voted for, such as reducing waiting times in the NHS, expanding funded childcare, ensuring that more pupils receive free school meals, more social homes, supporting transport and crucial maintenance work in our schools and hospitals. In many respects, these were priorities that were seen in the manifestos of other parties during the election period.

 Our intention is to invest £145 million in our health system to speed up access to treatment and to improve outcomes for the people of Wales. We are giving priority to providing additional funding to tackle child poverty and to help people across Wales with increasing costs of living, and are allocating an additional £55 million to start to deliver our commitment to transform childcare. This budget also provides £2 million to start to implement the Cynnal pilot scheme. That''s an important first step in our broader mission to tackle the terrible scourge of child poverty. We are investing an additional £15 million in free school meals. That will enable us to start to extend provision from September onwards to pupils in secondary schools.

 We are also providing £40 million in additional capital to improve the standard of school buildings, and a further capital sum of £5 million will be provided towards the community facilities programme. The budget includes £2 million to help to introduce the new swimming and water safety programme for schools, and another £8 million to continue to provide £1 transport on buses for children and young people, and also £2 million in a bus service between north and south Wales along the western corridor, which doesn''t have a train line. Everyone in Wales deserves a safe home that they can afford. That is why we are investing £20 million in capital to provide over 100 additional homes for social rent.

 The first supplementary budget is a routine part of the annual budgetary process. It provides assurances to our public services. It also ensures that members of Senedd committees in their scrutiny work can see clearly the changes to Government decisions in terms of income and expenditure. At this point, I would like to thank the Finance Committee for considering this supplementary budget in full and for publishing its report overnight. I have read the report this morning and I see value in its recommendations and I will try to respond positively to them, and I look forward to collaborating with the Finance Committee on refining our scrutiny and the budgetary process over the period of this Senedd.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22863&l=en', NULL, 115.5, 'High', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system; Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; social homes; social rent; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.460339', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767959', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('adf150520fa87a47c2f9', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', 'Thank you. So, I represent Bangor Conwy Môn, one of the largest towns is Llandudno, and we have the largest bed space, really, across Wales for the tourism industry. We are not suffering from immigration, I can assure you. We''re suffering from too high business rates and all the things I''ve mentioned in my speech. I can honestly tell you we are not suffering from immigration. Immigrants are not taking people''s jobs.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Janet Finch-Saunders', '', '212', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=10733&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.757463', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768295', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ae44779eb7e47dd3a41d', 'oral_question', 'Question not reached in Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '<p>The town-centre taskforce will address the structural challenges facing town centres, including business rates and planning reform, by working collaboratively with partners and maintaining a strong focus on delivery to help town centres adapt, attract investment and activity, and thrive in the years ahead.</p>', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', '', 'Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '', '', '', 'Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 90.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; planning_system', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'Housing minister', '', 'business rates; planning reform', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:06.997721', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=QNR', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ae686c2f00ae3943fff2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Thank you for asking me to speak on this really important legislative consent motion this afternoon in relation to the UK Labour Government''s steel nationalisation Bill. As a Labour member, as a trade unionist, support for our steel workers is very much in my DNA. My colleagues here today and my predecessors in the Senedd have always strongly supported steel workers in Wales, and we will continue to do so, whatever it takes to stand up for steel and protect thousands of steel-making jobs and communities in Wales. Steel is, of course, as many have said, one of the most important industries in Wales, whether in Port Talbot, known as ''steeltown'', or in Llanwern in Newport, where generations of families have built their lives around the steel industry.

 The steel industry has, of course, been a major employer for decades in the town of Port Talbot, and thanks to the prompt actions of the UK Labour Government in 2024, although, of course, we recognise that, sadly, the last blast furnace was closed down, there was hope, and there still is hope, in the form of a transition period and funding that has saved jobs. With £500 million of UK Labour Government investment, as part of a £1.3 billion deal into steel transition in Port Talbot, we were able to protect 5,000 jobs and ensure that Welsh steel making has a new future with a new electric arc furnace being commissioned, and, importantly, workers have had a seat at the table during the transition period. Working in social partnership with our unions, the Welsh Government and the UK Government invested in helping workers find new opportunities and new careers, with Port Talbot becoming a model for a worker-centred just transition. Instead of leaving people behind to fend for themselves, unions, the Welsh Government and the UK Government invested in helping workers find new opportunities and new careers.

 Now, as the daughter of a steelworker, I know that when change comes to a steelworks, it affects far more than the payroll. It affects confidence, it affects family life and mental well-being. That''s why the support that''s been offered to workers throughout this transition has been so important in helping people to actually navigate that uncertain period with dignity and hope. Without the support provided through the community support centre and the wider transition board programme, many workers would have faced redundancy with far fewer options on the table. The success of that model has helped people move into retraining, access advice and find new opportunities while remaining rooted in their community. We''ve also not seen any increase in unemployment benefits claimed during the transition, which is testament to its success.

 Now, turning to this LCM, I know there''s been extensive engagement with the Department for Business and Trade, the Bill''s sponsor, the Wales Office and this Welsh Government, and I understand that this Bill is primarily being brought forward to address the current situation at British Steel Limited in Scunthorpe. The UK Government has no plans to use the powers in this Bill more widely, and I know that concerns have rightly been raised about the powers being used in Wales at a future date. However, I''m reassured that the UK Labour Government has listened to these concerns about any potential impact in Wales and has set out a number of commitments in response. Firstly, DBT has confirmed that the UK Government would consult Welsh Ministers in advance of using the powers of this Bill in any undertaking in Wales. So, it''s worth noting that this was accepted, of course, by the Scottish Government as being sufficient assurance to support the LCM in Scotland. Secondly, it goes further by confirming that, should the UK Government ever have to nationalise a Welsh steelworks, the UK Government would identify and ensure an appropriate role for the Welsh Government in the oversight of the works, and it would do this while recognising the statutory role of the board and the Secretary of State in those circumstances. And it also reflects the established practice of the UK Labour Government in terms of steel projects in Wales. For example, the Port Talbot transition board established to administer more than £100 million of UK Government funding to the steelworks and businesses affected by the transition is deputy chaired by a Welsh Government Minister—now, obviously, the Minister, Adam Price.

 So, I am satisfied that the UK Government has now addressed the Welsh Government''s requests, and I now urge Senedd Members to support the LCM on this Bill. I support this LCM today because it''s not about ''if'' and it''s not about ''when'' these powers are ever going to be needed in Wales, because of the assurances I have set out above and, actually, many of the comments as well that have been made in the Chamber so far.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21119&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'LCM; Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.323023', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767919', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ae786946c04ffa644ed2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'This Bill is critical to supporting such a key asset. Primary steel plays a critical role in so many parts of our economy, and maintaining the ability to produce it underpins so many key sectors. My constituents know this more than most, with generations of experience and knowledge in this industry. While this Bill is welcome, and I support the Gweinidog''s recommendation to grant consent, I have to say, given the importance of steel to the Welsh economy and given our recent experience, it''s extremely disappointing to see how little time both the Senedd and the Welsh Government has had in terms of genuine dialogue with the UK Government on the implications of this Bill and the role the Welsh Government would play in the event that this Bill would be used in a Welsh context. I welcome the concession won by the Gweinidog, and we may hear a bit more about that later. However, people in Port Talbot have been let down by the previous Conservative Government and the soon-to-be previous Labour UK Government under Starmer. 

 Members of the sixth Senedd will remember Luke Fletcher''s calls for the UK Government to bring the Port Talbot site into public ownership and retain primary steel making throughout the transition. At the time, those proposals were ridiculed and dismissed as fanciful. Yet, here we are today, discussing the implications for Cymru of a UK Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill. It''s abundantly clear, regardless of which party is in power at Westminster, what was deemed necessary for Scunthorpe was apparently too much to ask for Port Talbot. What assurances can the Gweinidog give to workers in Port Talbot that this Government will continue to make the case for greater investment at the Port Talbot site under a Burnham-led UK Government?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elyn Stephens', '', '12164', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21006&l=en', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'LGHP Committee members', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.312556', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767913', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('aee4ea4dac00d4fd00bc', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee inquiry', 'Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties', 'Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties

This is a committee inquiry. Inquiries take written evidence, and a call for evidence is an opportunity to influence.

Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties Follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties Issue Details Issue History Inquiry2 The Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee has agreed to conduct a follow-up inquiry into Empty Properties in Wales. The original inquiry which concluded in December 2019 was conducted by the Fifth Senedd’s Equality, Local Government and Communities (ELGC) Committee. Empty properties includes residential properties, heritage buildings, and commercial or other non-residential spaces. Terms of reference To consider: >>>> >>>progress made towards implementing recommendations made by the ELGC Committee in its October 2019 report ; >>>the impact of actions taken forward by the (then) Welsh Government as outlined in its December 2022 update and identify where further progress is needed; and >>>any new policy developments since the ELGC Committee’s initial inquiry. <<< Evidence gathering: The Committee will hold oral evidence sessions with targeted stakeholders early in the autumn term (dates tbc). Business type: Committee Inquiry Reason considered: Senedd Business; First published: 23/07/2026 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47957', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 189.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; call for evidence; empty properties; inquiry; oral evidence; terms of reference; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:52.702342', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47957', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('af34b520cc970d3d10e2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '10. Voting Time', 'We will now move to voting time. Unless five Members wish for the bell to be rung, I will proceed directly to voting time. The first vote is on item 8, the legislative consent motion on the Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill. I call for a vote on the motion, tabled in the name of Adam Price. Open the vote. Close the vote. In favour 86, no abstentions and seven against. The motion is therefore agreed.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '10. Voting Time', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26555&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.864667', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767982', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('afbfa5465d9fccaee30c', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'I didn''t know—. The clock wasn''t running in my original contribution.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26313&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.846772', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768003', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b10969a19bd2f2f4e9af', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'I''d like to start by thanking everybody who''s contributed to the debate so far. As a Port Talbot boy, I don''t think I''ve ever heard Port Talbot mentioned so much in political discussion; I only wish it was on a more positive note. Before I go any further, I''d like to declare about members of my extended family that some still work in the steelworks and some have been forced to seek alternative employment.

 I stand today in support of the legislative consent motion for the steel industry Bill. As the Cabinet Minister said, steel remains a vital component of our economy and our communities. I believe that this piece of legislation should be given consent so that steel can play its part in securing Wales''s future. However, as has been mentioned, we cannot ignore the timing. For my home town of Port Talbot, this legislation has come too late. The blast furnaces that defined generations of Welsh steel making have already been extinguished, and the loss of those highly skilled jobs and the impact upon families and the local economy have been profound.

 I support this Bill today because we must do everything possible to protect what''s left of our steel industry in Wales and give our workers hope for the future. Steel is not just another economic sector, it is a foundational part of our construction, manufacturing and infrastructure projects across the country. Losing any more steel capacity would be a serious blow to our industrial base in Wales.

 I am therefore urging the Welsh Government to work with Westminster to ensure that any nationalisation delivers genuine long-term security for the remaining workforce in Wales, invests in modern technology and helps to maintain high-skilled employment in the south Wales Valleys. The people of Port Talbot deserve more than managed decline. They deserve a viable, financially sustainable future in the industry that they helped to build. I''ll be supporting this motion today, but I''m doing so with a clear message: we must learn from what happened in my home town. Future decisions regarding strategic industries must put jobs, skills and community stability first. Diolch yn fawr.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Benjamin Hodge Mckenna', '', '12142', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21589&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.386969', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767882', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b23e2f7eb626225fcf0b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'We currently provide support for those in fuel crisis, as you know, through the discretionary assistance fund, and also through the support that has been given to the Fuel Bank Foundation to build their network in Wales. That includes enhanced support for those using off-grid fuel in light of the impacts on energy prices arising from the conflict in the middle east. It''s excruciating, isn''t it, to see the actions of President Trump again today—no ceasefire, the terms in which this conflict is still being allowed to escalate. We know the result of that, especially in terms of fuel poverty, especially in terms of what that''s going to do to the fuel price cap, what that''s going to do in terms of inflation. It''s going to hit the most vulnerable people in Wales the hardest.

 We are already making sure that we are providing crisis support, but I know that the level of the price hike is already unaffordable. It is something that I worked closely with your former colleague Mark Isherwood on in the last Senedd. As I said, the Prif Weinidog has called on the UK Government—. Many of the levers around this are things like the social tariff, which is something that this Government would really like to see, which would really help those families in need who are finding their fuel bills unaffordable. He''s asked for a four-nation summit to ensure robust plans are developed to support low-income households with those unavoidably high energy needs and who will be most impacted by higher energy prices when winter approaches.

 As regards the Warm Homes plan, that sits in the portfolio of my colleague the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning, if I''ve got that right. But, of course, fuel poverty is very much in my portfolio, so we''ll be working together on those plans, and I know there''s already action afoot as regards the Warm Homes plan.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=1606&l=en', NULL, 132.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'energy_efficiency; welfare_and_support', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Warm Homes; discretionary assistance fund; fuel poverty', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.605043', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766843', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b2b5dfc49ff1eb365e8c', 'oral_question', 'Oral Question', 'Oral Question - OQ64418', 'What consideration has the Welsh Government given to updating national planning policy to make it easier for ...', 'https://record.senedd.wales/OralQuestion/64418', '2026-07-08', 'Helen Jenner', '', '12153', '', 'Bangor Conwy Môn', 'Senedd (tabled business)', 'Tabled on 08/07/2026', '', '', NULL, 25.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning policy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:47.881560', 'record_search:type=oral_question:query=planning', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b300f0bca9c060a2dc7a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Members may note that the electronic clock is not working.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23405&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.521637', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767952', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b4eb10b5b6d5e1fc2087', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Thank you, First Minister, for that response. As one with direct recent experience of the impact that childcare costs can have on family budgets, I was so pleased to stand on a manifesto that committed to introducing a transformational childcare scheme. I''m also pleased to see the Government proceeding immediately to invest in the childcare sector through the supplementary budget, and to start the work of giving every child the best possible start and allowing parents to return to work. That''s the importance of facilitating the passing of the supplementary budget, so we can proceed immediately. So, I would welcome more information on the next steps that the Government will take to expand provision.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Mair Rowlands', '', '5084', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=3262&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.477529', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767563', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b50788d41074b46004fc', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '10. Voting Time', 'Item 9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27: For: 44, Against: 49, Abstain: 0

 Motion has been rejected

 Click to see vote results', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '10. Voting Time', '16085', '', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.869810', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767988', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b59d3601293281ebc4eb', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you, Deputy Presiding Officer. Minister, England''s Building Safety Act 2022 provided leaseholders with remediation orders and cost protection since 2022. Wales has only now delivered those same powers through the 2026 Act, and secondary legislation remains out to consultation until September—four years behind England, with only four out of 161 buildings being fully remediated. Given that England ran this exact same consultation process first, is the Welsh Government using the evidence and models already established to assess what''s needed here and finally speed up the process?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3805&l=en', NULL, 143.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', 'Consultation open', 'building safety; consultation; leaseholders; remediation orders', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.045331', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768153', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b6496eba126a12db9961', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I don''t want to use many words, you''ll be glad to hear. The truth is that tourism taxes are common worldwide, including in Amsterdam, Venice, Barcelona and Paris, some of the cities with the greatest influx of tourists in the world. And of course, by now, there are great parts of England that have this tax—and in Scotland as well—including Liverpool and Manchester on our doorstep. The tax does not penalise the tourism sector, rather it boosts income for local government to improve our areas, to reinvest, to improve visitor amenities in the area, improving the feel of our tourism destinations and therefore facilitating sustainable tourism.

 We as a party have long understood and appreciated the importance of tourism to our economy—I can say that as somebody who had family in the sector—and we take pride in the fact that we can show the beauty of our country to people from across the globe, but this must also bring benefits to those who host the tourists and our communities. One way of ensuring this is by introducing a tourism tax. What''s important to note, at a time when many people speak of devolving powers to local government, is that it''s up to our local authorities to introduce the tax, not the Welsh Government. If a council doesn''t think it''s a good idea, they don''t have to introduce it, but this ensures that they have the power to do so, if they wish, to help with the cost pressures that they face.

 This fund needs to be used to improve infrastructure, to enhance the tourist experience—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Elwyn Vaughan', '', '8371', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=13885&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'tourism tax', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.963165', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767096', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b76418ab21a36c283c4a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'Diolch yn fawr iawn. Thank you for those questions. Again, some of those last elements on funding are not necessarily relevant to legislation. On water, yes, I do believe absolutely in the principle of public ownership of water. That''s my starting point always. We have now quite an exciting process that we are following with positive engagement from the UK Government currently, on handing us the ability to control and regulate our water in future. Legislating on water isn''t necessary to be part of this, if you like, first 18 months of legislative programme, because we are involved in those negotiations with the UK Government now on empowering ourselves in Wales. But, legislation, we will look at how we can use law to protect Welsh water in future because it is so, so important to us.

 And on community right to buy, I appreciate the support there for that principle. There''s a lot of work to be done in defining what exactly, for example, should be included. Some of the work has begun already, but we will get down to that work in earnest. I should have answered the question earlier on the question of funding. There is already a range of grant and loan funding arrangements available that community groups can access. I''m always interested in seeing how those can be developed further, but I think the important thing here is laying down the legal framework within which we can identify who can be the responsible bodies locally; which community assets need to be protected; and how we put those protections in place. Let''s get that work done first.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10789&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community assets; community right to buy; legislative programme; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.231582', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767790', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b8971f1027509358828d', 'legislation', 'Welsh Statutory Instruments 2025', 'The Building Safety Act 2022 (Commencement No. 6) (Wales) Regulations 2025', 'The Building Safety Act 2022 (Commencement No. 6) (Wales) Regulations 2025', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2025/1368/2025-12-12/data.xml', '2026-06-18', '', '', '', '', '', 'legislation.gov.uk', '', '', '', NULL, 45.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Property & energy', '', '', 'building safety', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:15.207054', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2025/data.feed', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b8b7fcdac4bcfbaf7d57', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Thank you, First Minister. Of course, the risk with it being cross-government is that no single Minister actually holds on to the responsibility. Now, that was the distinct advantage of having a Minister for north Wales. This week, the Welsh Government has been pretty much silent in north Wales and about north Wales whilst these fires have taken place. More people may be left homeless in weeks and months to come with further extreme heat predicted, and that means that businesses will also be hit, likely events will be cancelled. Can you assure people across Wales that your Government will support businesses and home owners who are impacted by wildfires and extreme heat?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Ken Skates', '', '267', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=1275&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'homeless', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.307112', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767502', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b9579ed410c28126251b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I strongly support this motion from my colleague Llŷr Powell. The tourism sector is not just a luxury for Wales—it is a lifeline. It contributes £3.8 billion to our economy and accounts for over 11 per cent of employment. This so-called visitor levy is the wrong tax at the wrong time. It is regressive by design. A £1.30 charge on a £50 room represents a far higher percentage increase than the same charge on a £200 room. It hits cheaper accommodation hardest, precisely the kind used by families, younger visitors and domestic tourists, the people who keep our smaller businesses alive. Smaller operators, often family-run, already face heavier administration burdens than the large chains. This levy imposes more paperwork, administration costs and responsibility. It is not progressive; it is a tax on aspiration.

 Our accommodation providers are already struggling. Visitor numbers haven''t recovered to pre-COVID levels. Cost-of-living pressures are squeezing domestic demand, whilst international visitors have cheap choices elsewhere. Visit our coastal towns and you will see the evidence: tired frontages, peeling paint and far too few high-quality new developments. If budget operators could simply raise their prices without losing occupancy, they would have done it years ago. Instead, many are fighting to survive.

 These businesses are already heavily taxed and regulated. They collect VAT, they pay business rates, they pay corporation tax, they pay national insurance and they pay income tax on behalf of their staff. They generate additional revenue for the Government through alcohol, tobacco and waste charges. Yet now we ask them to become unpaid tax collectors once again, this time for a levy that, in practice, frees up council budgets for other spending rather than delivering genuine new investment in tourism.

 Let me be frank about the numbers in my own area. Conwy council carries £230 million in debt, with annual interest and repayments exceeding £17 million. Conwy council''s own impact assessment estimates that the visitor levy would raise £2 million a year. That''s a sticking plaster. It''s not a solution. Council overspending and poor financial management—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'John Clark', '', '12169', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=13046&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'business rates; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.873109', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767098', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('b9811c2d9f98dcd218ee', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'First Minister, you''ve had months to prepare for your legislative statement today, for this moment, in this Chamber, and I have to say, is this it? Is this all you''ve got to offer the people of Wales? You tried to present yourself as a Government in waiting before the previous election, yet it''s quite clear that you don''t have a very well-developed plan as to what you actually want to do and deliver for the people in Wales. Tribunal reform, tinkering about with the Welsh tax system—hardly the stuff that people on the doorsteps during the recent Senedd elections were concerned about.

 You had absolutely nothing to say about the Welsh NHS, which we know is on its knees, nothing to say about the Welsh education system, which is delivering the worst educational outcomes in the UK, very little to say on the Welsh economy, which is lagging behind other parts of the United Kingdom. And your statement, of course, was made in the sort of casual and presumptive way that we''ve become familiar with. It seems to make an assumption that your legislative programme will get the support across the Chamber that you want it to get. But if I can just remind you, you''re a minority Government, right? You can''t make assumptions without engagement with other political parties prior to making your statements. Not engaging with the media prior to making your statements, but engaging with other political parties and trying to seek a little bit of consensus.

 Now, we''ve made it clear, right from the outset of your term of office, that we''re prepared to work with you, we''re prepared to work with any political party in this Chamber, to support legislation that is in the interests of the people of Wales. But you''ve made no attempt whatsoever to reach out to my party, and, to my understanding, either Reform or the Labour Party, about this legislative programme that you''ve set out today.

 So, let me set out some alternative proposals on the things that are a matter of concern to the people of Wales. The NHS is in crisis. Some of that crisis is down to failing leadership. So, we need an NHS accountability and governance Bill to ensure that senior leaders in our health service are competent and can be held to account when things go wrong. We need to deliver that mobile phone ban in schools that you''re, sluggishly, going to consult on, without giving a clear commitment as to how you''re going to introduce it. We need a new additional learning needs and autism Bill to help young people to reach their full potential. And we need to protect those female-only spaces in schools, leisure centres and other buildings to keep women and girls safe and to make sure that that Supreme Court ruling on sex is fully implemented.

 We do need a national development agency, and this is one of the things that I hope we can work together on. But it needs to be a reformed development agency that will deliver true benefits for the people of Wales. And it''s not just a national development agency. We also need to reform business rates here in our country so that we can revive our high streets, and help shops, pubs, cafes and post offices to be able to thrive and to prevent the further closure of buildings in our town centres.

 And while we do support the community right-to-buy plan, which we''ve been calling for for years, by the way, in this Chamber, because we do want to protect those community assets that are at risk of closure, what about legislating for another right to buy—housing? Why don''t we restore the right to buy to help people get onto the housing ladder—a reformed right to buy that puts the proceeds back into developing more affordable housing? Why don''t we legislate to bring empty homes back into use so that we''re not having these terrible empty properties plaguing our towns and communities? Why don''t we take some action on council tax to help people keep their council tax costs down, with referendums for excessive rises?

 Now, I heard what you had to say about this rural-proofing legislation that you want to bring forward, something that we welcome. But, in your 100-day plan, you specifically refer to funding in relation to that. You didn''t mention funding at all today. And, of course, we all know that we actually need wider reform. It''s not just rural areas. There are certain geographic parts of Wales that are missing out on fair funding. North Wales, mid Wales, west Wales—they''ve not been receiving a fair share of investment under successive Welsh Governments as a result of your budget deals with the Labour Party, and we need to change the law to make sure that they do.

 So, I am surprised at the lack of cross-party engagement in advance of your statement today. I don''t think your statement is sufficiently ambitious, and, if you want some ideas, I''ll send you a copy of our manifesto so that you can implement that instead. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Darren Millar', '', '171', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=9795&l=en', NULL, 137.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; housing_supply; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system; Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'affordable housing; business rates; community assets; council tax; empty homes; empty properties; legislative programme; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.100674', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767758', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ba97dd9c32236c73c6ce', 'calendar', 'Senedd forward look', 'Legislation Committee — 14 September 2026, 13.30', 'Legislation Committee is scheduled to meet on 14 September 2026 at 13.30.
Meeting status: Confirmed; Hybrid
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=986&MId=16253', '2026-09-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '16253', '', '2026-09-14', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Legislation Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:40.392888', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bc694fd3cc5de8898482', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'I''m grateful for the question. I would respectfully ask the Member to reflect on the code of conduct on pursuing our own particular cases within the Welsh Parliament. But, of course, it is right that we all represent people who have been waiting a very long time on waiting lists. It is the length of waiting lists in terms of numbers and it is the length of time that people are waiting on them that have led this Government to conclude, even before we came into Government, that this was a priority. It''s why we prioritised in the last budget, for 2026-27, the need to invest in the NHS now. It''s why decisions have been made through the supplementary budget, which we hope can be passed today, in order to spend on bringing down waiting times and making the system sustainable, because it is unacceptable. Wherever people are—and that, of course, includes people here in this Siambr who are on waiting lists—it''s not acceptable that we believe this to be normal, and that is why we are placing the utmost seriousness on the work of working with health boards, making the investment, having those performance reviews so that we can build a sustainable NHS and care system for the future.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=563&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.229423', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767497', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bc946c1795c585aac221', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'I can predict what you''re going to say now, but just let me read what your leader said in March 2025 in response to the then finance Minister, Mark Drakeford.

 ''The Cabinet Secretary is suggesting that by voting against a flawed budget as a whole, Plaid Cymru is voting against individual spending decisions.''

 ''this is a spurious argument.''

 ''What we''re saying today is that whilst we can agree with elements of the budget...this is a flawed Labour budget and that is why we''ll vote against it today.''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Ken Skates', '', '267', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26127&l=en', NULL, 44.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.820980', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767985', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bd0ab2864d6e15ba2455', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '3. Early business and forward work programme: committee''s remit and responsibilities', 'Moving on, then, to item 3. So, the next item outlines the committee''s remit and responsibilities. If members have detailed questions about the remit, it may be more appropriate to discuss these in more detail during the session later on, in private. But, for now, are members content to note the remit? Yes, happy.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16163', '2026-07-08', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '3. Early business and forward work programme: committee''s remit and responsibilities', '16163', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16163?startPos=84&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:18.014123', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16163&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766763', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
