Current stage: Royal Assent (27 April 2026)
Stage history: Bill introduced 03 Nov 2025; Stage 1 13 Jan 2026; Stage 2 05 Feb 2026; Stage 3 10 Mar 2026; Stage 4 17 Mar 2026; Royal Assent 27 Apr 2026', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46599', '2026-04-27', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd', '', '', '', NULL, 130.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'second_homes_and_short_term_lets; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', 'Bill progress', 'Development of Tourism; Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act; Regulation of Visitor Accommodation; Royal Assent; Stage 1; Stage 2; Stage 3; Stage 4; visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:18.484612', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46599', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bf57f0f9196a9293f665', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Voting Time', 'Motion NDM9284 as amended:

 To propose that the Senedd: 

 1. Recognises the vital contribution of the tourism sector to the Welsh economy, directly supporting more than 150,000 jobs and contributing to 5 per cent of Wales''s gross value added. 

 2. Celebrates Wales as a world-class destination, welcoming visitors from across the globe. 

 3. Welcomes the Welsh Government’s support for the tourism sector by:

 a) promoting the visitor levy as a choice for local authorities, that can enable new investment into local tourism; and

 b) delivering the Government’s commitment to keep the 182-day occupancy threshold for self-catering business under review and create clear and reasonable exemptions where a property would not qualify as a private home.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Voting Time', '16084', '', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', 'self-catering; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:17.141412', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767138', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bfa56c432eb2665f4061', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Short Debate: Health inequalities in the south Wales coalfield', 'With a population of around 750,000, almost a quarter of Wales''s population live in the south Wales coalfield areas, covering much of the Valleys, extending from Carmarthenshire in the west to Torfaen, the eastern valley. This area has been slower to recover from the decline of the coal industry than many other former coalfield areas in Britain. The former coalfield community in my constituency extends down from Blaenavon to Pontypool, with a population of almost 25,000. Pontypool, my bro fy mebyd , and communities across Torfaen are proud places with a strong history and identity. Post-industrial communities like these have yet to fully recover from Thatcher''s de-industrialisation. Austerity has only made this worse.

 In this area, inequalities are clear to see. They''re a living scar. To highlight some of the inequalities, I''ve seen data that compares the two council areas within my constituency. These comparisons are not intended to dismiss the very real challenges faced in Monmouthshire, but to illustrate the inequalities that exist between neighbouring communities.

 Cancer incidence in the Torfaen coalfield wards is higher than the rest of my constituency. Similarly, chronic conditions are 5 per cent more common in the Torfaen wards. Mental health conditions are present in a third of GP cases in the Torfaen wards. Premature deaths and long-term illness are much higher in Torfaen than in Monmouthshire. So too are personal independence payment and universal credit claimants. This makes the residents in Torfaen much more vulnerable to UK Government benefit changes. But every statistic represents a person who may be struggling to find work, or a family who may be struggling with the health of one or more of its members, a child going to school hungry.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Matthew Jones', '', '12151', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Short Debate: Health inequalities in the south Wales coalfield', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=15784&l=en', NULL, 22.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'welfare_and_support', 'Housing system', '', '', 'universal credit', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:17.154599', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767147', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c160e323ddf98c2e65c7', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'Deputy First Minister, your Government has just announced a further £55 million for childcare, including additional funding to expand Flying Start and increase capacity. Expanding Flying Start childcare to all two-year-olds was a commitment made by Welsh Labour and Plaid Cymru under the co-operation agreement, yet that expansion is still being delivered in phases. Before asking the sector to deliver a further expansion of funded childcare, can you tell us whether the Welsh Government has identified exactly how many additional childcare places and how many additional childcare practitioners will be needed to deliver those ambitions, and, if so, will you publish those figures? Diolch yn fawr.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Helen Jenner', '', '12153', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=50&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'co-operation agreement', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.442193', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766795', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c32d86327df9383dbbfa', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', 'Thank you very much for raising an issue of real importance, something I know that many in this Senedd are concerned about and are passionate about. Certainly, there was a clear commitment in the Plaid Cymru manifesto—and that strong commitment remains within Plaid Cymru—to ensure that the community right to buy does become law. I''m not going to be attempting to make any statements before we hear from the First Minister later this afternoon, but, certainly, I hope that that unambiguous commitment on the importance of that has been made clear by me this afternoon.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=7687&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community right to buy; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.889087', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767737', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c3324b331000dbbff0be', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Ophthalmology services', 'Diolch, Ddirprwy Lywydd. Minister, it''s difficult—it is always difficult—to follow Gwyn. In some ways, he''s always been my warm-up act, but I''ve always been a flop. [ Laughter .] Some of the people that spoke today have been absolutely fantastic with their lived experiences, and it shows the depth of the problems that exist. In a very minor, minor way, I ended up having to go to England to have an operation about eight years ago, because my eyesight got so bad for a number of reasons. I had new lenses put in and I just couldn''t work. It reached the point that I couldn''t work. So, these things really do matter, and they improve life dramatically.

 A few things about Gwyn. Gwyn, obviously, knows exactly what he''s talking about, the same as Safa, and what possibly people also know is he''s really passionate about what he does. However, as an ophthalmologist, he is one of the top people that you would ever get. He travels internationally to show how to do things, and he does also go abroad to help wherever he can. So, in that respect, what he''s pushing here really, really does need support, and I''m glad everybody so far has come this way.

 I''m going to be a bit of a damp squib. Everybody''s talked so well, and, just like James said, there''s no real point in pushing those things over and over and over again. What I''m going to do is bring it down to brass tacks. The whole thing, apart from being absolutely fantastically wonderful to do it, it is essentially something that''s going to save the NHS money, it''s going to produce huge amounts more productivity, and, as a lovely by-product, it''s going to affect so many people''s lives.

 Plaid Cymru have a fantastic part in our manifesto, which is essentially the hubs—the place where specialists can stand alone beyond all the battle that''s going on in the NHS, where they can focus, and they can rely on predictable methods, because people are coming in, everything''s controlled, which lead to the productivity gains. These are the simple things that really change. What you do is you get more people through, you get more pleasant working, because it isn''t that stress that you''ve got in accident and emergency, and people can focus on the specialisms, and therefore everything increases in terms of the ability to do what you can do.

 Substantial productivity gains offered by surgical hubs, which is what we''re proposing, Minister, is something I''ve experienced quite recently. My wife had an operation, and the ability to move through the process is so slick. It''s like a production line; that''s the only way I can describe it. So, it actually does work. Implementing this across Wales is, without doubt, the solution to the problems that we have. Surgical hubs also mean there''s less outsourcing; we''re saving money. I had to go private when I went to England to get it done, but other people will have to—. Previously, Labour have been paying quite heavily for treatment to be outsourced and for people to have operations privately but paid for by the NHS, which isn''t anywhere near a long-term solution. Staff retention in the NHS and in this particular area as well is massively difficult, and the very fact that you''ve got good working conditions, you''ve got high productivity and happy people means that you''re going to have better productivity just as a result and people will want to stay. Safa mentioned in a talk a few days ago that, of a cohort of seven ophthalmologist doctors coming into, I assume, Singleton Hospital, within a very short time, four of them had left, just because the systems were dysfunctional, they weren''t happy. And it really does make a difference.

 This doesn''t just affect health, but also can affect what we do outside. You''ve got people who are far more productive. With me, I could not work, I couldn''t drive, and from knowing that I couldn''t even see colour, all of a sudden, being able to see colour and a clock on the wall was mad—totally mad. But these things do change people''s ability to be productive so, so much and it had a huge impact on me.

 The brain drain that we''ve got because these doctors are leaving here, we just cannot allow to happen. In my opinion, the hubs that we have and the ways and the systems that we have to work will facilitate people to be happier and to stay.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'John Davies', '', '4650', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Ophthalmology services', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=10562&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'hmos_and_standards', 'Private rented sector', '', '', 'damp', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.558046', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767045', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c3bb50e7c6c6472a5a8b', 'oral_question', 'Question not reached in Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '<p>We committed in our manifesto to exploring opportunities for further permitted development rights to allow community-led housing and make it easier to convert empty buildings on high streets and spaces above shops into residences. This will be achieved through changes to regulations.</p>', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', '', 'Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '', '', '', 'Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'permitted development', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:07.005933', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=QNR', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c4ef95fc67e80f9b3d2f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I think we can all agree that Wales is very much a world-class destination for tourism. That moment when you see someone posing for a photograph outside a castle or looking around at many of our museums or enjoying our national park—that is a special moment, and it should make us all proud. Because, of course, Wales does actually belong to all of us, and we''re very lucky that we can share our beautiful country with people from all over the world. I often enjoy having family over and taking them up to Pen y Fan and getting them to do that early morning walk up the mountain, and, of course, the many lush waterfalls that we have.

 But getting back to our capital city for a moment, we''ve had some incredible success lately in putting Cardiff on the map. In just the last decade, we hosted the UEFA Champions League final, which smashed the previous footfall record by bringing more than 310,000 people into our great city. We hosted Clash at the Castle, a real global event, and the company''s first stadium event in the UK for over 30 years. Most of these spectators came from outside Wales, and when they were surveyed, they said they just wanted to explore a bit more. Last year, we had the European Rugby Champions Cup, and with the eyes of the world on them, Oasis chose Cardiff to kick off their reunion tour. All of these major events bring thousands and thousands of people who want to spend their time and money in Wales, and this brings me to the topic of this debate.

 The Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025, which only became law last year, isn''t even coming into effect anywhere until next April. So, we haven''t had a chance yet to assess the positive impact that it can have on our communities. To be clear, this gave councils the choice to introduce a small charge on overnight accommodation after they had consulted with local residents and businesses. In Cardiff, we have chosen to introduce the levy, and it will come into effect next April. Anglesey, Conwy and Gwynedd councils are currently consulting, and they will make their decisions later this year. The challenges and the opportunities around the visitor economy will ebb and flow, and all councils are free to make their decisions in the future. And that''s exactly the point here: this is a great example of devolving power beyond the Senedd, taking decisions closer to the people who live there.

 Reform would have you believe that this is a tourism tax—they call it so, and they say that it''s going to cost people in the future. On this idea that it is going to deter people from coming to Wales and spending their money here, the truth is that this is a visitor levy where local councils choose to adopt it and decide. It''s £1.30 for a night. You can''t even get a pint or a coffee for that much money. That''s what we''re talking about here—a very small contribution from people who have chosen to visit Wales, which will collectively make a real big difference.

 Councils who adopt the visitor levy will spend that money on improving their tourism offer, protecting local nature and landmarks, and promoting Welsh culture. Surely these are objectives that we all support. Most importantly, the people who choose to come to Wales will be happy to support them too. This is a way of making sure that our visitor economy grows and the benefits feed back into our communities, creating good jobs, revitalising our towns and cities and protecting Welsh culture for future generations to enjoy.

 This is all about making sure that the infrastructure works as well. This Government''s amendment makes reference to two specific policies—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12736&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', 'Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy); tourism tax; visitor accommodation; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.834114', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767081', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c5e0bb62632a08a6a9a2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', 'I want to see more money in our NHS. You could put more money into the NHS if you listened to us and stopped funding some of your vanity projects like planting trees abroad, like some of the projects that you''re doing close to home, wasting Welsh Government money looking at Welsh independence, something the public overwhelmingly did not vote for. That''s the money that could go into the NHS. We''ve also heard about ''small change'' for overseas offices. That''s money that could go into our NHS. That is the reason why we voted against your supplementary budget, because it''s a bad deal for Wales.

 You just waffled then in your answer to me, Minister, because you didn''t actually say when we were going to have those two-year waits gone. You''ve said ''months''. You''ve mentioned elective care hubs. By the time they go through planning, by the time the Welsh Government shuffles paper around their offices, that''s going to end up being years. You know how the system works. You sat on committee with me when we discussed these things. They don''t come overnight. So, can you now outline a date when two-year waits will be eliminated in Wales, because far too many people have been waiting in pain? You promised people you would sort this out, and it''s about time you started to deliver, because your honeymoon period cannot go on forever.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=824&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.750725', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768059', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c6f14f3c1f7371816ea6', 'legislation', 'Senedd Act', 'Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025', 'Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025
Current stage: Royal Assent (18 September 2025)
Stage history: Stage 1 01 Apr 2025; Stage 1 25 Nov 2024; Stage 2 15 May 2025; Stage 3 01 Jul 2025; Royal Assent 18 Sep 2025; Royal Assent 08 Jul 2025', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=44788', '2025-09-18', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd', '', '', '', NULL, 130.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'second_homes_and_short_term_lets; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', 'Bill progress', 'Royal Assent; Stage 1; Stage 2; Stage 3; Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy); visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:25.205236', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=44788', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c738e2730f1c61a09232', 'legislation', 'Acts of Senedd Cymru', 'Building Safety (Wales) Act 2026', 'Building Safety (Wales) Act 2026', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/2026/5/2026-04-28/data.xml', '2026-06-25', '', '', '', '', '', 'legislation.gov.uk', '', '', '', NULL, 84.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Property & energy', '', '', 'Building Safety (Wales) Act; building safety', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:14.611174', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/data.feed', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c78ea9e504e87e8ee0d5', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'We could have a philosophical debate here about how many months is months. All I can say is that all the actions that have been taken by this Government within the first 100 days—. You know, commissioning that work on establishing the surgery hubs, on making sure that we put that investment through this supplementary budget that we want to get passed today, so that we can, both in capital spend and in resource spend, make that investment to drive down those two-year waits—. The actions that Government is taking are aligned with bringing down those two-year waits as quickly as we can. We want to do it in months. I not so much stick to that—that is precisely what we set out to do before coming into Government, and what we are actively delivering now in Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=937&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.266257', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767474', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c83565da51d2d40cffb5', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Budgets are about priorities, they''re about choices, and I''m afraid that this supplementary budget gets those things wrong. As the Cabinet Minister herself said, this budget honours the previous Welsh budget to the decimal point. And that''s the point. That''s why we disagree with it, because we disagreed with the previous Welsh Government''s budget as well, for the same fundamental reasons that we disagree with this one. You bleated on yet again today about the £300 million in-year pressures that you are facing as a Welsh Government. Well, that''s because of the budget that you signed up to as a party in the last Senedd earlier this year. So you can''t bleat about it when you fixed this previous budget from which you and the Welsh public and our Welsh public services are now facing the consequences.

 And I''m sorry, but it really does make me irate when I hear people complaining about decisions to invest in our defence industry to keep our country safe, which is what you implied earlier on about the decision being taken by the UK Government. I''m no friend of the UK Government, but I support investment in our defence industry because we need to make sure that we keep our country safe. And what we really need to do to pay for that is to cut welfare bills, get people back into work, and people off the benefits system.

 We all know that you''ve got over £400 million to spend, £327 million-worth of that is due to additional learning needs spend in England, yet not a single penny in your supplementary budget was being allocated to that spending line today. And I know there''s been some discussion between you and the Labour Party, but not a single penny in your original supplementary budget proposals was being allocated to additional learning needs. I think that is appalling. I really do think that''s appalling, especially when all of us in this Chamber, especially those who''ve been here for a long time, know full well the pressures that those schools are facing, know full well the pressures that parents are facing not getting the support for their children. It''s not good enough, and we need to shift the dial.

 Patients need the dial shifting too, and we need some honesty from the Minister about the funding for the NHS. You''ve talked about the additional resources that are available for health in the supplementary budget, but the reality of this budget before us today is that the NHS budget is still being cut in real terms. That is shameful. You''re cutting the NHS budget at a time when demand is rising and pressures are growing when compared to the previous financial year. It''s a statement of fact, First Minister. Would you like to get your calculator out? I''m happy to lend you mine if you want.

 People in Wales want to be treated in a hospital bed, not a corridor. They want an ambulance to come on time if they pick up the phone and it''s an emergency. They want to get an NHS dentist. They want to be able to get an appointment the same day that they phone their local GP. They can''t at the moment, and that''s why we need to invest in our NHS, not cut the NHS budget, which is what your supplementary budget is still allowing for, even though there''s extra cash available that you could be allocating.

 There''s also very little in this budget to help people with the rising cost of living. Where''s the action to help people keep their council tax bills down, which have been spiralling out of control year in, year out? Where''s the support for businesses in Wales, who are struggling with those sharp increases in their business rates? Where''s the support for pubs? Where''s the support for our high streets? Where''s the extra finance for our hospice and palliative sector, which is crying out for sustainable funding? And where''s the support for our farming industry as well? We need to make sure that supplementary budgets reflect the views and the wills of the people of Wales. But most of all, you''ve got that fundamental problem with the original budget, which you signed up to earlier in the year and you''re now carping on about. We''ll be voting against this supplementary budget today.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Darren Millar', '', '171', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24391&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates; council tax; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.634423', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767899', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c86850a076de493ad078', 'legislation', 'Senedd Act', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act 2026', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act 2026
Current stage: Royal Assent (01 April 2026)
Stage history: Bill introduced 19 May 2025; Stage 1 04 Nov 2025; Stage 3 03 Feb 2026; Stage 3 11 Dec 2025; Stage 4 10 Feb 2026; Royal Assent 01 Apr 2026', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=45883', '2026-04-01', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd', '', '', '', NULL, 136.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'homelessness; social_housing; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Housing system', '', 'Bill progress', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation; Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act; Royal Assent; Stage 1; Stage 3; Stage 4; homelessness; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:23.127470', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=45883', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c9667ed684abdacd2285', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much. We are already taking action through the social housing grant and the transitional accommodation capital programme. This will tackle homelessness, reduce reliance on temporary accommodation and ensure that people across Wales have access to quality homes that are energy efficient. We''re also establishing Unnos to accelerate the delivery of social housing across Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5604&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'housing_supply; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'Unnos; homelessness; social housing; temporary accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.231002', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768213', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c9ed53ae3c6b9cb83341', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you for that question and for your interest in this particular area. During this Government''s first 100 days, we will be making a full announcement on the purpose of the new taskforce that we''re currently establishing and its membership. Of course, it''s the taskforce that will consider its specific remit, and I''m very eager for that to happen, rather than us, as a Government, setting out certain criteria. It''s the people in that taskforce, in consultation with others, that will set the remit for that work. But I assume, having been involved in this sort of work for years, that looking at business rates and how we can make sustaining a shop in a town centre more attractive than being located in an out-of-town centre would be one area that they would look at, along with planning and how we can accelerate the process in order to convert vacant buildings in our town centres. But there''s also this desire to look holistically at what public services can be brought into our town centres and how we can accelerate the planning process around that too. There''s a great job of work to be done, but it''s very exciting, and I look forward to working with the taskforce.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4734&l=en', NULL, 115.5, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'Housing minister', 'Consultation open', 'business rates; consultation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.149829', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768157', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('caa7057df6b5de076f3c', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Diolch, Lywydd. I''d like to thank all the Members who contributed to this evening''s debate. Let me begin with a concern that is widely shared across the Siambr: the way that this Bill was handled, which did curtail our ability to scrutinise it. The preceding legislation that led to the Scunthorpe operations being effectively put into Government control and then nationalised, of course, was an England-only Bill and it was passed, as I said earlier, in a day. This was a four-nation Bill. It relates not just to a potential move in relation to that one site, but it''s a piece of legislation that could be applied to steel operations anywhere in the United Kingdom. Given that, I think the approach should have reflected the devolution settlement.

 There was no prior engagement with the Welsh Government. The reason that has been given, I understand, is one of it being commercially confidential. Well, with something that is as strategically important to the future of the Welsh economy and the UK economy, then I don''t think that stands up to scrutiny, to be perfectly frank.

 We were put in a difficult position, as were the other devolved legislatures. The Northern Ireland Assembly hasn''t been able to arrive at a position because there''s been simply no time because of the compressed timetable. We, as we always will, will reflect on what we could have done differently, but, in essence, we were put in a very, very difficult position.

 What we tried to do was, given that suboptimal position, we tried to get the best result for Wales. Yes, the commitments that we sought in relation to Wales''s legitimate interests on behalf of our steel communities—we wanted that in statute, that those are our principles. I think they''re principles that will, I think, probably be widely shared right across this Siambr. Now, we were able, because we held out, to get those commitments, at least on the record, and we were successful at least in getting a commitment from the current UK Government. But if they were prepared to make that promise at the despatch box, why weren''t they prepared to put it in statute? Because the promise doesn''t change, the commitment doesn''t change, but, of course, a statutory commitment is always better because you can hold not just this Government to account, but, of course, it stands, then, in the legislation governing future administrations as well, and I simply don''t understand the resistance to putting it on a statutory basis.

 People have spoken rightly with feeling about Port Talbot, and I will not add further to what I said in opening, except this: we cannot restore what that community lost, but we can make certain that the option to nationalise is firmly available to future Governments if necessary, and that''s what consent today achieves, and it''s why, in all conscience, we could not do anything other than recommend to the Senedd that we support it. Of course, any policy in relation to nationalisation or wider steel strategy has to be fair, and we have to have fair competition, and I think we were right and other Members are right to raise that. Public ownership exists to rescue an industry, not to distort a market, and if these powers are used in Scunthorpe or anywhere else, we will measure the consequences against the interests of our Welsh steel industry, our Welsh steel companies and communities that depend on that sector, and we''ll not stay silent if Wales is made to pay the price of an intervention elsewhere.

 But our message today is simple: we consent to this Bill, or we urge the Senedd to do so, because Wales must never again be told that the tools to save our steel do not exist. We consent to this legislation with our eyes open and with our conditions on the record: equal treatment, fair competition and a full Welsh voice whenever these powers touch the interests of our people. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Adam Price', 'Cabinet Minister for Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy', '5030', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21747&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion; devolution settlement', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.401306', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767941', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('cb5c07c2e0c79c946bc7', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Welcome back. We now move to item 9, debate on the first supplementary budget 2026-27. I call on the Cabinet Minister for Finance to move the motion—Elin Jones.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22625&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.439437', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767937', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('cc01dc816e8f42ead919', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Go on.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26311&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.845088', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768001', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('cd463452918c10b9ec2e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', 'The Member will be aware that the previous Government set a number of targets and failed to reach them because they weren''t realistic. We are absolutely realistic in how we are investing this money into improving outcomes so that people are seen quicker. We know that we''ve got issues around gynaecology. We know we''ve got issues around cataracts. We know about orthopaedics. So, these are the things that we need to look at and improve. That''s why we''re going to set up the elective care hub, so that we can see people quicker, so that people can get their treatment within the time frame that is required, so that they improve and can get on with their lives. So, we''re absolutely clear. We''ve been clear all along. I''m not quite sure where these questions come from. Everything we''ve said in the past, we are sticking by. We will be achieving this in a matter of months. However, let me remind you that you and your colleagues and others here voted against the budget yesterday, which had £145 million in order to tackle this. So, let''s not have any crocodile tears that you are concerned about waiting lists. Clearly, you''re not. If you were, you''d have supported the supplementary budget so that we could get this money onto the front line to see people as soon as possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=756&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.743264', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768055', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('cd4e8f2e0b4a09a829d4', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Llywydd, I move the motion and open the debate on the Welsh Government''s first supplementary budget for 2026-27. This supplementary budget is our first opportunity to propose changes to the full budget this year and to start the important process of ensuring that expenditure aligns with our priorities as a new Government. We are focusing, therefore, on a targeted allocation of resources and additional capital. This will enable us to make swift progress on our most important priorities as a Government, while also demonstrating that money is being managed prudently and that there is stability for public services and partners.

 Alongside this, as is usual in supplementary budgets, the budget makes formal adaptations to the general level of funds available to Wales in light of the spring statement and the main objectives of the UK Government for this financial year. It also includes a restatement of this year''s final budget to reflect the new ministerial portfolios. Moving a first supplementary budget is an early mechanism in the financial year to set out formally changes to the annual budget. The second supplementary budget is the final opportunity to finally formalise the allocations towards the end of a financial year.

 This first supplementary budget therefore makes £433 million of additional allocations. This includes £294 million for Welsh Government priorities, and £139 million for budgetary responsibility transfers. The balance of £246 million in unallocated resources and £29,000 in general capital has been held in reserves in the first supplementary budget in order to manage in-year pressures and to respond to priorities. This unallocated funding is our protection against the financial position that we have inherited and against the significant financial pressures that are unfunded within the year, including a total of over £300 million of overspend that the health service is currently reporting. Managing these in-year pressures is a significant challenge, and that challenge is made more difficult because of the tight funding framework that we work within. The unallocated resources funding is there as reserves to be allocated later to help us manage the current pressures in the system, if necessary. We cannot allocate that funding in its entirety so early in the financial year. The previous Government made similar allocations to the NHS in supplementary budgets at the end of the financial year: £200 million in 2025-26, £424 million in 2023-24.

 The Welsh Government''s settlement is directly linked, as we know, to decisions made by the UK Government. It can change, even in-year, up and down. Only a few days after I appeared before the Finance Committee, the UK Government announced its defence investment plan. That is expected to lead to a direct negative consequence for our capital budget this year. If the cut of 1 per cent anticipated hits all departments of Government equally, that will mean the loss of between £25 million and £30 million immediately during this financial year alone, and there is more to come.

 This is a supplementary budget.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', 'Cabinet Minister for Finance', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22645&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Finance Committee; final budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.441935', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767932', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('cf22873644c93120ea99', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '5. Member’s Legislative Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): A Bill on corporate parenting', 'I understand that Government say the voluntary charter is better because it can reach private and third sector bodies that legislation cannot, but let us be honest about how private organisations behave. They follow the law not a leaflet, so the charter''s breadth is precisely its weakness—it reaches widely because it demands less. Keep it for the bodies we cannot compel, but for our health boards and NHS trusts, the bodies we can bind, a care leaver deserves the duty the law enforces, not a promise quietly set aside. We do not leave safeguarding to goodwill, so we should not leave corporate parenting to it either.

 The Counsel General leaned heavily on one specific defence: that part 6 of the code of practice is being revisited with a new chapter on corporate parenting to be laid later this year. I must tell the Chamber that this defence is a dead end. By law, its scope is strictly limited. It applies solely to local authorities exercising social services functions. It cannot legally impose a single duty on a health board. 

 Local authorities are already under a statutory corporate parenting duty. This achieves absolutely nothing for a young person failed by the NHS. The Government cannot expand the scope of this code; only primary legislation passed by this Senedd can. And I hope no-one offers me yet another review, because, in 2023, the Senedd''s own committee looked at exactly this, and asked a question titled, ''If not now, then when?'' Three years on, we are still answering ''not yet''. The legal reality is clear: the most robust mechanism is an Act of the Senedd, one that either imposes duties directly or requires Ministers to bring regulations that we can subsequently shape together.

 Section 23 of the English Act already provides wording that could be adapted for our devolved bodies. The power is ours; it was always ours. No duty is entirely immune from judicial review, but that risk is not managed by inaction, but by drafting duties clearly with ascertainable standards and robust targets. That is a reason to legislate carefully, not an excuse to avoid legislating at all. The risk we face by doing nothing is we are simply paying different parts of the system to manage a crisis we failed to prevent. We are not saving money by relying on voluntary charters, and far, far, far more importantly, we save a life from avoidable suffering, because this, above all else, is not a monetary question, but a moral question. These are children who do not have what we had. The average young person in Wales leaves the parental home at 24, but many care leavers are forced out at 18—it is a sheer cliff edge that I would have faced—with no parents to catch them when they fall.

 Leaving care is a predictable route into destitution, with one in four care leavers ending up homeless. The Children, Young People and Education Committee asked us, ''Would any of us claiming the system works be happy for our own child to be cared for by that system?'' I certainly would not. Would you?

 I will be honest about the journey this motion has taken to reach the floor today. When I first tabled it, I did not feel the broad support across the Chamber. But that has since changed, and I am genuinely deeply grateful to those who broke rank, did the right thing and put their names to it. I single none of them out, except to thank them sincerely. But I would gently say this: the moral case for strengthening care-experienced children''s rights did not suddenly become true last week. It was always true. The reaction to this week has shown that the public appetite for this is massive. It is the very reason they need statutory duties the system is legally bound to honour, not a voluntary promise it may choose to keep.

 Some may ask whether this is the moment, whether, amongst all the pressures on the Senedd, this is the priority. But my answer to them would be: if not now, then when? For a care-experienced child at the cliff edge, there is no ''later''. Their childhood is not on hold while we find a better time. Each year we wait is another year of young people falling through a gap we already know how to close. After all, it is not just about words, but about actions, signalling to care leavers in law that they matter. Sometimes you do have to legislate in order to send a signal to care leavers that their rights are enshrined in law, and I believe that that is really significant, no matter what the voluntary charter says. Remember, the law is our living word, our living promise, and the law, ultimately, outlasts us all.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Sarah Cooper-Lesadd', '', '12167', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Member’s Legislative Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): A Bill on corporate parenting', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=6312&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'homeless', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.044036', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766955', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d03bc6356fc29d38c0f4', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'It''s not a surprise to me that a new party to this Senedd, Reform, would question why consequential funding is not merely passed on to the equivalent recipient in Wales, that this Senedd is merely a go-between. It suits their politics to neutralise this institution. But those of us who are pro-devolution, pro-standing up for the independence of this Senedd and our democratic right to make our own choices, I would expect us all to agree that it is for Welsh Ministers in this Senedd to decide how the Welsh budget is spent, and nothing should be presumed for Wales from an allocation happening in England to deal with English matters. For Wales, do not see England. From those early days of Edwina Hart as finance Minister and Rhodri Morgan as First Minister until today, this principle has been upheld by all Welsh Ministers and Governments.

 Now, I accept that some of you here who have called for the allocation of extra money related to ALN in England to be allocated to ALN in Wales may still support the right of this Senedd to take its own decisions. But I do say in response to that: beware of blurring these lines and diluting this principle. It''s been well defended up until now, by my predecessors and yours.

 As a Government, however, we have listened to the genuine calls made by parties in the Senedd, councils and schools to increase the allocation for ALN this year, and have been developing a strategic approach to tackle fundamental challenges, as well as responding to immediate pressures. On the table, in real time, in this Chamber, in this moment is £40 million this financial year of additional funding for additional learning needs, which focuses on activities that are deliverable, including direct funding to schools to stabilise and shore up current provision, and which establishes a coherent national model for local authorities and schools to develop local responses in a consistent way. This allocation will be followed by £40 million next year and the following year to provide some certainty to the sector—a £120 million total package for ALN to ease the pressure on schools and councils and to place ALN on a more sustainable footing for future years.

 Passing this supplementary budget today will enable us to have assurance over the allocations to the outlined priorities of £294 million. That will enable us as a Government to carry a smaller risk in committing to allocating £40 million additionally to ALN this year. We will regularise this allocation in our second supplementary budget and include the allocations of £80 million to future annual budgets. If Members vote against or abstain on this budget today and it doesn''t pass, the Government will be able to press ahead with the majority of our work and spending on our priorities at our own risk. But, due to the inherited in-year pressures, we will be unable to add to it another risk of £40 million spend. We will then only be able to use the funding allocated by the previous Government in its budget to ALN this year, with no £40 million uplift. However, we will continue to prepare for better future provision for ALN as part of our next year''s budget planning.

 I ask Members to think carefully, in casting your vote today, of what can be gained and what can be lost. There''s £164 million new spend on the NHS, childcare and travel opportunities for your constituents. There''s £130 million additional capital money into schools, hospitals and housing. There''s a £120 million package for councils and schools for ALN uplift. And to Labour specifically, we offer all of this and your previous Government''s budget inherited by Plaid Cymru, its allocations honoured and wholly untouched. This supplementary budget is a good deal for this Senedd and, more importantly, it''s a good deal for the people of Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23144&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.489039', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767974', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d0f68d97ad42d58e6181', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much. Yes, I do agree with you. That is why I''m eager to work with the Wales Pension Partnership to consider what further steps we can take together to strengthen the contribution of local government pension funds to Wales and to the kinds of investment that we want to see within our own country.

 I''m talking specifically here of homes again, because I do think that social housing is one of those issues that you''re referring to, which is not only the right thing to do, because we want to accelerate the process of providing more social homes, but there is also a consistent income flow coming in through rent from social housing. Therefore, it makes sense in general terms too for this investment here in Wales. I''m very pleased to hear that contribution from you supporting what we''re doing within Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3398&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.009900', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768139', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d2657f5d60d30d9b6239', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '3. When will the Welsh Government update ''Planning Policy Wales''? OQ64366', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister; Other committee chairs', '', 'Planning Policy Wales; planning policy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.157890', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768141', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d331ec82fb015527a8f1', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Could I just remind Members—? I understand that people want to contribute. We also want to hear the speakers, and people outside this Chamber are watching proceedings. We need to hear what the speakers are saying. Darren Millar.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24374&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.631336', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767898', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d4198b612a4460351268', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Ophthalmology services', 'Sight loss has profound consequences for individuals, families and communities, affecting a person''s independence, ability to work, education, mobility, mental well-being and participation in everyday life. Preventing avoidable sight loss is about maintaining independence, reducing health inequalities and enabling people to continue living healthy, fulfilling and productive lives. That is why timely access to eye care remains such an important priority for this Government.

 The national strategy remains the agreed blueprint for transformation of eye care services. It sets out a model based on regional working, workforce transformation, clinical networks, digital enablement and more effective use of capacity across the whole system. But the challenge has been and is implementation. Patients experience better care only when strategy is translated into delivery. I want a renewed focus on implementation and outcomes. Work is under way to establish a clearer understanding of progress across Wales, identify variation and provide a stronger basis for future delivery. I expect clearer implementation planning so progress can be measured against defined priorities, milestones and expectations.

 Digital transformation is essential to future success, ensuring that we rectify the mistakes of previous years. Better information sharing and integrated digital services will support safer, more efficient and connected care. We''re encouraging health boards to work more collaboratively, building on the regional work already under way across parts of Wales. Working with NHS Wales Performance and Improvement, health boards in Wales are increasing activity. Many organisations are treating six or seven patients on a cataract list as standard, closer to the Getting It Right First Time expectations. This national approach will ensure services benefit from improved capacity and more consistent pathways, supporting timely delivery.

 We must provide staff with the facilities to do what they do best—to see and treat patients. Our focus remains on building a system that can meet demand sustainably and deliver timely care. We''re launching an ambitious programme to expand elective capacity, developing up to 10 surgical hubs across Wales to focus on delivering the high-volume low-complexity procedures to reduce long waits for procedures such as cataract surgery, and it''s good to hear cross-party support for this plan. So, I look forward to seeing cross-party support for our supplementary budget in order to achieve this next week.

 Infrastructure alone is not the solution. Sustainable improvement requires workforce development, service redesign, digital transformation and implementation of the national strategy. Health boards need to share the available resources and work together on a regional basis.

 One of the strongest examples of service transformation is community optometry. Through Wales General Ophthalmic Services, optometrists with additional postgraduate qualifications are increasingly managing patients closer to home, improving access, allowing hospital services to focus on those with greatest clinical need. This approach is making a difference.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Ophthalmology services', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=11121&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.618745', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767052', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d55770e0cabd01824969', 'legislation', 'Acts of Senedd Cymru', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act 2026', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act 2026', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/2026/3/2026-04-02/data.xml', '2026-06-25', '', '', '', '', '', 'legislation.gov.uk', '', '', '', NULL, 91.0, 'High', 'digest', 'homelessness; social_housing; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Housing system', '', '', 'Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation; Homelessness and Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act; homelessness; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:14.612376', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/data.feed', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d74c47a177f3eb20e09e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'The Member opposite named a number of fantastic Welsh destinations in her opening remarks. None of the councils in those areas have confirmed plans to introduce the visitor levy. In fact, many have already ruled it out. And that''s the beauty of this legislation, that it enshrines local decision making. They have the freedom to consult, consider the evidence and make a local decision. Cardiff has chosen to do so. Other authorities are consulting on the possibility. But as Paul Marr put it, a one-size approach does not work. So, having local decision making is key, local democracy working as it should. I''m stunned that the party there and the party here don''t support that principle of local decision making.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=14276&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:17.010842', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767109', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('d7ea7b67052eb4ede54e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Statement by the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health: Priorities for Public and Preventative Health', 'Thanks for the contribution and for the welcome into the role. In terms of the bilaterals and the shared Government understanding, I think this is the first time we''ve ever had this discussion across Government level. I think there have been lots of pockets of good practice over a number of years, but nothing embedded, nothing strategic, no discussion about the impact of spend on the population of Wales in other portfolio areas.

 So, the bilaterals have been really good to kick that process off in terms of just seeing where we can work together. For example, you mentioned food and a discussion with the Cabinet Minister for rural resilience. That''s not just a bilateral between myself and that Cabinet Minister; we''re talking about education, we''re talking about our planning system, we''re talking about the economy and supporting our food businesses. So, that''s what this approach is absolutely about, to make sure we don''t miss those opportunities. And if we look at the approach to food during the last Government, it was quite narrowly focused, and this is absolutely building on the work that you mentioned by your previous colleague on the food Bill, to make sure that it is a whole-system approach.

 I absolutely agree with you in terms of the quality of food in schools, but also teaching our young people about food skills and making sure that we understand where our food comes from, supporting our brilliant agricultural industry, and looking at our procurement rules. So, this is not just something for food; this is absolutely health prevention, looking at the economy. So, those bilaterals have kicked off that discussion while we work out how we make sure that these are embedded discussions early on in every policy development—that we look at preventing ill health and reducing the health inequality that we''ve got and making sure it''s a cross-Government approach. And that''s my role—to make sure that we don''t miss those opportunities while we work out the mechanics, and that that is replicated on the official level as well. These conversations, potentially, have never happened before in these policy areas, so it''s making sure that that''s embedded so that it''s systematic and strategic that we really do look at health inequality and look at preventative health measures. 

 In terms of the budgets and the costs, you raise some interesting points there in terms of how that will be developed. You''re right in terms of a new budget line. I think the first step that we''re grappling with now is the definition of ''prevention'', especially if we are committed to increased spend on prevention over this Senedd term. How do we define that is really important—how do we define current activity within health and social care, but also in the other portfolio areas as we move to shift resources towards prevention. Getting that definition of prevention is absolutely key. There are different mechanisms then of identifying that spend. The Scottish Government are currently looking at how you tag some spend as prevention—so, not necessarily creating a budget line but tagging it as ''preventative'' and those definitions within preventative spend. We are exploring those options, with the key fundamental principle that transparency is key. Without us knowing what we''re spending, how we''re spending it, and, ultimately, what the outcomes of that spend are in terms of population health, it''s not as impactful as it should be, and that''s why I''m in this role. 

 In terms of the weight loss medication, absolutely—it''s creating that system of support around individuals for those lifestyle choices. They are right in some instances: preparing people for treatment in the NHS, for example, or certain body mass indexes. But making sure we''ve got that whole-system approach around the individual, to make sure there''s a sustained improvement, is really important, and we''re keeping that under review. Public Health Wales have been successful recently in co-ordinating a Wales-wide bid for funding through the obesity pathway innovation programme. That''s £8 million of funding in order to look at these issues, to develop and test approaches that could form part of that delivery model going forward. We want to be looking at treating obesity as a chronic condition, and, obviously, the changes that would mean in terms of how the NHS would respond to that would be key. 

 I''ve mentioned the food issue. On substance misuse, absolutely. I''m pleased to hear your approach in terms of the person-centred approach—not just dealing with the substance misuse issue, but, absolutely, mental health and making sure that all the services around that individual are there. Following on from the last Government''s harm reduction method, we will absolutely be taking that forward and working closely with the Deputy Minister for mental health. If I''ve missed anything, as I know time is tight—[ Interruption .] I''ll come back to you on the COPD screening specifically. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Nerys Evans', '', '261', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Statement by the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health: Priorities for Public and Preventative Health', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=15205&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', '', '', 'planning system', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.730268', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767911', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('da7b7d7785eb80a41cb8', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'Tourism in Wales is not a ''nice to have'' on the margins of our economy, it is one of the load-bearing pillars of it. Welsh Government''s own data shows that this sector directly supports more than 150,000 jobs—nearly 12 per cent of all employment in Wales—and generates £3.8 billion, 5.1 per cent of our gross value added. These are not abstract statistics on a spreadsheet; this is the guesthouse in Tenby, the cafe in Betws-y-Coed, the outdoor activity centre in Eryri, the small hotel in Llandudno that keeps its lights on in February because it knows summer is coming.

 In Pembrokeshire and Anglesey, more than one in five jobs sits within tourism. This is not a supplementary income stream for those communities; it is the backbone of their local economy. Four in five tourism enterprises in Wales employ fewer than nine people. This is a sector of family businesses and sole traders, not international hotel chains that can absorb bad policy and move on. When this Chamber legislates without understanding that reality, it is these people who pay the price. So, point one of this motion shouldn''t be controversial, it simply asks the Senedd to recognise what is already true.

 My colleagues were with the First Minister last Friday on a luxurious cruise ship in Holyhead port, and we were hearing about the good work that Cruise Wales and Holyhead Town Council have done to get more cruise ships to stop there on their British isles tours. There are around 60 a year at the moment, bringing close to £0.25 million to the immediate local economy, not to mention the wider benefits of showcasing north Wales to returning visitors. This is an excellent example of what can be achieved when Government works together with the sector, but these examples are few and far between.

 Wales is currently the only UK nation yet to recover pre-pandemic visitor spend. Scotland''s international spend was up 41 per cent on 2019, ours is still down. If we''re serious about Wales being world class, this Chamber must ask why we''re the exception and stop making decisions that make the answer worse.

 The first bad decision: tourism tax. A tax on visitors is a tax on the jobs that serve them, a blunt instrument that hits small, rural and coastal operators hardest. And remember that Wales, as part of the UK, already charges 20 per cent value added tax on hospitality accommodation, more than double Germany''s 7 per cent or France, Italy and Spain''s 10 per cent. Layer a visitor levy on top of this and we''re pricing Wales out of the market when we''re trying to grow the sector. It''s not even been implemented yet, but the perception of Wales taxing visitors, especially from England, I believe, has already had a negative impact on visitor numbers, and I''ll let my colleagues expand on this further.

 The second bad decision: introducing the 182-day rule. Nearly 40 per cent of genuine holiday lets are not making this unrealistic high bar of letting days. Independent survey evidence shows 47 per cent of owners are losing money as they''re being classed wrongly as second homes. England''s equivalent threshold is 70 nights, less than half ours. And there is no evidence so far that the higher Welsh bar achieves its aim of increasing the number of family homes in local communities. Protecting housing supply and community sustainability can be done through the planning system, not through an unfair fiscal tool, and I''ll let my colleagues set out why that number needs to change.

 We talk about sustainable tourism. What does that mean? Well to me, in my opinion, it means the visitor should bring maximum cash to the local economy for jobs and businesses, with minimum disturbance to daily life and no lasting change to the fabric of a community. But sometimes it feels there are winners and losers in our busy summers. The tourism sector makes the majority of their revenue over summer, but for those who can''t park outside their house or find their bins full of visitors'' litter, it can feel that they are not benefiting from this surge in numbers, and we totally get that. But this need not be the case.

 We can have winners and winners if we ease up on regulation. Let the farmer open his field to cars on busy weekends in certain hotspots; let residents associations negotiate a residents'' permit scheme, so locals get parking while visitors pay for theirs; let a village hall become the ticket office or shuttle bus pick-up point for a nearby attraction, taking a fee for the privilege. On this side of the Chamber, I have a whole bucketful of ideas to make sure that local communities benefit from this sector, but this doesn''t include taxing visitors, leading them to go somewhere cheaper, which could literally be a few miles away in a different county that hasn''t implemented the tax. Although I do not agree with the policy, if the previous Welsh Government wanted to implement the tourism tax, they should have had the guts to insist it was a Wales-wide policy rather than passing the buck to local authorities.

 Sustainability also means a wide range of accommodation, so people stay rather than just visit for a day in tourism-specific places. This means relaxing regulations to support more hotels, bed and breakfasts, caravan parks and campsites. For example, we recently changed the law in Wales. We only allow 28 days of permitted development for a landowner to run a summer campsite. In England it''s 60. Nobody will invest in water supply or temporary toilets for just 28 days of business. So, let''s change that.

 Sustainability means better transport and co-ordination around our tourism hotspots. I know, from a number of occasions, freezing cold at the base of yr Wyddfa waiting for the Sherpa bus that never arrived, that this system isn''t good enough. I know there''s a transport plan specifically for the Eryri National Park, but it currently remains a plan. Where is the Welsh explorer pass linking public transport to discounted attractions? Where''s the Cadw castle pass, where you can visit as many castles as you can in a week and take a selfie at each one and get a discount in the shop? This is not a lack-of-resources issue. These things are often policy and partnership problems. We can do so much more to support the sector without taxing it.

 And finally, I want to make this point about how the tourism and hospitality industry can make a vital contribution to stopping our rising youth unemployment. Almost everyone''s first job was in hospitality. It teaches communication, quick thinking, hard work. Major hotel chains and tourism attractions employ lawyers, accountants, engineers, sales and marketing. It''s not just making beds and serving tables, but for many of those best paid professionals, that''s where they started. And I''ve seen first-hand how hospitality careers are valued across Europe.

 I''ve visited Swiss hospitality schools, which are world class, drawing students from across the globe to prestigious courses. I recently set up a hospitality excellence fund with my local college, privately funded to help our students compete nationally to raise their game. We talk about ambition for Wales. So, why can''t we compete with those Swiss colleges? Why can''t we say proudly from the very top that hospitality in Wales is one of the best starts to a career? Where is the drive? Where is the ambition? Well, maybe it starts now.

 In this Chamber, by supporting this motion and removing those two barriers—one fiscal, one regulatory—that the previous Government has put in the way of that sector''s success, we need to be working cross-party to create an environment that encourages private business to invest in our tourism offer, high-value accommodation, world-class visitor attractions. Let''s liberate the tourism sector, not strangle it with taxes and regulation. Llywydd, I commend the motion to the Senedd.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Louise Emery', '', '12182', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=11702&l=en', NULL, 154.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; housing_supply; planning_system', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'holiday lets; housing supply; permitted development; planning system; second homes; tourism tax; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.688195', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767063', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
