Meeting status: Confirmed
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=978&MId=16232', '2026-09-16', '', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '', '16232', '', '2026-09-16', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:40.408237', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('dd57cadedfe369b92c2e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'As I''ve said, this is a supplementary budget. It does exactly that: it supplements a budget. This Government has honoured the previous Government''s budget to the decimal point in order to provide continuity of delivery for our public sector and other partners. We added to that budget, but took nothing away. As the people of Wales would have expected of us in this supplementary budget, we have sought to allocate funding available to us on the priorities they voted for in May''s election—on reducing NHS waiting times, on increasing affordable childcare, on tackling child poverty, on transport initiatives, and on a capital programme to improve school and hospital buildings and build new homes. I understand that Members, during the scrutiny of this budget, have sought to pursue the allocation of £344 million consequential funding for the debt write-off to English local authorities as a result of ALN historical overspends in England. This Government has sought to engage constructively on that issue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23080&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'new homes; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.479544', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767967', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('dd8084c811ded4362678', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Statement by the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health: Priorities for Public and Preventative Health', 'Thank you to the Deputy Minister for her statement today. As Welsh Labour''s spokesperson, I welcome the cross-Government approach to preventative health, but I''m interested in what outcomes will be delivered for people across Wales. You''ve mentioned today the designated preventative departmental expenditure line within the Welsh budget for 2027-28, but I''m sure you''d agree, Minister, that it isn''t just the creation of the budget line itself that we should be focused on, but rather how people''s overall health and well-being will improve. I, like many others, challenge the Government to go beyond the process. We want to know what exactly will change and how success will be measured during this Senedd term.

 I''m glad the Deputy Minister mentioned two very effective interventions: screening and vaccinations. It''s good to hear the Deputy Minister supports the continuation of the national lung cancer screening programme, which was introduced by the Welsh Labour Government. I''m surprised there''s no mention today of the HPV vaccine. Since it was introduced in 2008, the HPV vaccine has reduced cervical cancer rates by almost 90 per cent in women in their 20s, and that has saved hundreds of lives every year across the UK. So, I''d like to know how the Government will engage with schools across Wales to encourage participation in vaccination programmes like this.

 Cervical screening also saves lives, and there''s work to be done to make that more accessible. So, I''d like to see the Government learn from areas where there have been increases in screening uptake. You did give one example of screening uptake best practice, but I think there have been some other ones, particularly in London, where GPs have adapted to offer appointments for their clinics that fit in with people''s work and family lives, such as on weekends and late evenings. So, I''d be interested to hear if you''ve been able to look into that.

 Encouragement to help break down stigma is also key to increasing screening uptake. So, I''d like to know how Public Health Wales is focusing screening delivery that is tailored to local communities, for instance consistent breast screening opportunities in each geographical area throughout the year to support local health board demand. There''s also work to be done engaging with businesses and organisations to support their workforce to attend all screening as a priority.

 Targeted communications and education is important, breaking down stigma for everyone, but specifically for those attending their first screening appointment. We all know the way people access information is changing, and I''d like to know how national screening programmes are adapting to fit into those communication methods to reflect this.

 Inclusion health is a globally recognised approach to address and prevent the health inequalities facing marginalised groups who struggle to access health services and experience far poorer health outcomes. We know that there''s a huge human cost to these inequalities, as well as a cost to public services such as blue light services, A&E and emergency inpatient treatment. A stark example is people experiencing homelessness who experience acute physical and mental health problems and die 30 years younger than the general population.

 There are some fantastic examples of inclusion health work led by Public Health Wales and services such as Cardiff and Vale inclusion service, and the partnership work between Ysbyty Gwynedd and Cyngor Sir Ynys Môn. However, this is often reliant on passionate individuals going above and beyond the remit of their roles, rather than inclusion health being prioritised, systematic and integrated with health services. Prioritising inclusion health is key to preventative health, as well as delivering significant savings to emergency health services. So, will you commit to making inclusion health one of your ministerial priorities, and consider developing an inclusion health strategy?

 Finally, we know that sport helps to tackle obesity and to prevent physical and mental health conditions. Professional and grass-roots sport clubs play a leading role, and their impact is far-reaching. They are trusted places at the heart of our communities. I''d also recommend to you the Fit Fans programme, which works across our football league clubs. In Plaid Cymru''s manifesto, you said you would invest in sports and physical activity as part of the preventative health agenda, and you''ve mentioned today your discussions with the Minister for culture and sport. However, any new work stream does need funding. The health minister couldn''t give an answer when I previously asked how much that investment will be and where the money will come from. So, can you, Deputy Minister, today tell us that? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Jayne Bryant', '', '5038', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Statement by the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health: Priorities for Public and Preventative Health', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=15485&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'homelessness', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.774790', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767924', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('df9a2b38c24dc0b3321e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'I ask Members to vote for this budget motion, to lift our collective heads above tribal party politics. Because this is a progressive budget for progressive parties. And even more importantly, it puts the priorities of the people of Wales first.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26453&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.861298', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767978', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e0545199789dd18e4252', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'It''s great to see that this Government is tackling the housing crisis, supporting housing affordability and increasing the rights of renters. In 2025, in my constituency, over 65 per cent of the population were priced out of housing in Gwynedd, and the median price on the Llŷn peninsula is more than £400,000, which is unaffordable for 96 per cent of local people. The impact this has on the area is very severe. Young people are leaving their localities, moving to the city or across the border, which greatly harms the local economy and culture. It''s good to see the Government doing this essential work, and I''m also really pleased to hear the news about the development of the right to adequate housing. This right is fundamental, and having a law on this would be transformative for thousands upon thousands of people across the country. So, what will the timetable be for bringing the aforementioned measures and the community right to buy to the Senedd?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Beca Brown', '', '12186', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=10545&l=en', NULL, 137.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'rent_controls_and_affordability; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'adequate housing; community right to buy; housing affordability; right to adequate housing; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.200293', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767768', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e0a3022094ff1281cd32', 'oral_question', 'Oral Question', 'Oral Question - OQ64114', 'What action is the Welsh Government taking to tackle youth homelessness in Caerdydd Ffynnon Taf?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/OralQuestion/64114', '2026-06-04', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', 'Caerdydd Ffynnon Taf', 'Senedd (tabled business)', 'Tabled on 04/06/2026', '', '', NULL, 35.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'homelessness', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:46.897679', 'record_search:type=oral_question:query=homelessness', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e11123da97c725ae5303', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Well, I do, indeed, and I''m giving you some latitude.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26304&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.843116', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767999', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e32d5e6fc37a59cc5ea3', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I did so for a very specific reason. Over 20 million people visit Cardiff every year, and we wanted to grow that number further, whilst also recognising and responding to the pressures that those visitors bring to the city. Over decades, Cardiff has built an enviable reputation as a destination for major sporting and cultural events. Every summer, the city teems with live music in our stadium, our castle and our parks. The new indoor arena, now under construction, will bring a further 1 million visitors every year to the city. From the Champions League final that Shav referenced to the Tour de France to the European football championship in 2028, a team Wales approach between Welsh Government, Cardiff Council and other partners has allowed Cardiff to secure some of the world''s premier sporting events over the years.

 I know from my unsuccessful attempt to purchase a ticket to see Oasis how much visitors are prepared to pay to purchase these golden tickets. If you''ve travelled from North America to see Taylor Swift, my guess is you''re not going to worry about paying a visitor levy of £1.30.

 Here''s the mad thing, for all the visitors that come to Cardiff, often as part of events that the council is delivering, for all the money they spend in local bars, restaurants, hotels, for all the overnight clean-up operations required after 70,000 people have partied in the Principality, the council receives not one penny of income, thanks to the completely centralised taxation system we have in this country. 

 A visitor levy, estimated to raise about £3.5 million a year for Cardiff, starts to change that calculation so that the council raises revenue from major events to in turn fund a better experience for visitors and residents alike. And it will also allow Cardiff to attract new events to the city and also better market ourselves internationally. That was the calculation in proposing a levy.

 In the extensive consultation carried out with key stakeholders and the general public, of the 2,000 responses received, 62 per cent supported those proposals, recognising how the money would be ring-fenced to support the visitor economy. It''s not a tourism tax, it''s an investment in tourism. It''s a modest contribution from visitors towards services, infrastructure and attractions that make Wales such a desirable place to visit in the first place.

 Elwyn Vaughan references—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=14092&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; tourism tax; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.987798', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767117', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e3780ecf3a9e4ced40f1', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', 'So, the issue with the graduates lies squarely on the shoulders of the previous Government. This is an issue that was under your watch, back in 2023. As you said, these students were the cohort going through from 2022-23, and it will take a few years to work through the system. That''s why one of the first things that I did when I came into this post was set up a graduate summit, in order to try and find options for as many of these young people as possible. Because here in Wales, different from England, they are streamlined, so a promise is made to them. So we need to try and honour that promise, and it''s disappointing that we were left in a position where those promises were broken by the previous Government. So now we''re trying to find a long-term solution for this. That''s why we''ve asked Healthcare Inspectorate Wales to work with the health boards, to work with the representative bodies, to bring that plan together, and I expect to see a plan by the end of December—or early autumn, in fact.

 Now, when it comes to the bursary scheme, once again, may I just remind you that this wasn''t part of any negotiations that you brought forward or discussions that you brought forward for the supplementary budget, which you voted against as a party, in order to improve health outcomes in Wales. I am absolutely more than happy to discuss any options that you might have and consider their impact and assess how they might be included, but I just remind you that, as we have been informed by the Cabinet Minister for Finance, there are massive in-year pressures currently in the health service, and those pressures are only going to get worse as time goes on because of settlements coming down from London and the fact that we don''t have a fair funding formula for Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=1214&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.785911', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768044', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e3f2a43362df62e27830', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', 'Well, where do I begin? I''m just really pleased that we''ve had such a good discussion and I just want to first of all really welcome the comments by the Minister. You found me nodding away and I was clearly in a lot of agreement with what you were saying, because the truth of the matter is that we have had a range of different schemes. Some of them have worked and some of them have still had gaps, whether those are the ones that we created here in Wales or whether they were created elsewhere.

 The big thing for me, in and amongst everything that I''ve heard from everybody, particularly the younger Members of the Senedd, is that we need to understand that this is about the social and economic issues and bringing them together. If we are not going to be doing anything very real in relation to building more homes that are affordable, addressing the issues around renters'' rights, for example, if we are not going to be tackling the big stuff around climate change and so forth, there isn''t going to be a future for anyone. And I worry, more often than not, about my own children, and even when I was first even considering having children, I was thinking, ''Am I doing the right thing?'' And now I look at my kids, one’s 15 and one’s 13, and I''m thinking, blimey—. Now I get to be in this building. And my daughter keeps reminding me, ''Mum, it''s because of you lot that we''re in this situation.'' So, I get it from home on a daily basis, so when I walk in—. Look, for me, what I would say, and I''ll ask the Minister, is: can you please report back to the Senedd after the discussion that you have with Milburn? Many people that I have spoken to, from lots of different organisations, including the TUC themselves, have said that it''s good that we''ve had that review, but there were still gaps even there. And I think that there''s clearly more work to be done.

 I just want to say there have been lots of comments made about immigration, and we can talk about these things all day long, but the data doesn''t stack up. And I think that I very much do welcome the fact that we have also heard, of course, from Janet, who made it very clear it''s about business rates. And I agree. We need to review business rates. There''s more work that needs to be done there. Actually, immigration has been a good thing for both Wales and for the country as a whole.

 What I would say is that, in terms of public procurement, specifically—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=11645&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.848706', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768304', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e4513bc82c96a5106bc2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'As you update ''Planning Policy Wales'', can you confirm your intention, therefore, to make the planning system more permissive so that we can build the homes Wales needs and, indeed, build the industrial developments that Wales needs? Will you make it more permissive, or will you continue to risk letting the not-in-my-backyard brigade stop developments and delay developments that have overwhelming public benefit?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5161&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'Planning Policy Wales; planning policy; planning system', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.188393', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768184', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e6b5a206345c9b58c1b1', 'press_release', 'Welsh Government — Announcement (notification)', 'New Welsh Government announces its first phase of legislation', 'New Welsh Government announces its first phase of legislation
The First Minister Rhun ap Iorwerth will set out the first phase of the Welsh Government''s new legislative programme in a statement to the Senedd this afternoon.

 The programme includes legislation to strengthen the rights of tenants in the private rented sector, initially focusing on strengthening the enforcement framework and the regulatory role of Rent Smart Wales, as well as requiring the sharing of rent data. This will lay the groundwork for more substantial changes to improve housing affordability, make rent fairer and limit no fault evictions later in the Senedd term. The Welsh Government is also developing proposals to legislate for the Right to Adequate Housing, and will proceed with a community right to buy scheme.

 Read the announcement: https://www.gov.wales/new-welsh-government-announces-its-first-phase-of-legislation', 'https://www.gov.wales/new-welsh-government-announces-its-first-phase-of-legislation', '2026-07-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', '', '', '', NULL, 234.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; rent_controls_and_affordability; evictions_and_possession; rent_smart_wales; homelessness; social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Rent Smart Wales; adequate housing; community right to buy; evictions; housing affordability; legislative programme; no fault evictions; private rented; private rented sector; rent data; right to adequate housing; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:54.659031', 'mailbox:senedd-monitor@nrla.org.uk:FIXTURE-005', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e7a519ef7565f7146618', 'oral_question', 'Oral Question', 'Oral Question - OQ64366', 'When will the Welsh Government update Planning Policy Wales?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/OralQuestion/64366', '2026-07-08', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', 'Brycheiniog Tawe Nedd', 'Senedd (tabled business)', 'Tabled on 08/07/2026', '', '', NULL, 35.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Planning Policy Wales; planning policy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:47.876424', 'record_search:type=oral_question:query=planning', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('e8f4103cdff65a5b553b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Lywydd, diolch yn fawr. I move the motion and recommend that the Senedd gives its consent to the relevant provisions in the Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill as set out in the legislative consent memorandum.

 Steel is no abstraction to Wales, it is in our bridges and our railways, in the turbines that will power our future and in the history of generations of families from Shotton to Llanwern, from Trostre to Cardiff. It''s a strategic national asset, and a country that cannot make its own steel is a country living at the mercy of others. That is why this Bill matters. It builds on the UK Government''s steel strategy and it provides the power to transfer the shares or property of a steel undertaking into public ownership where a public interest test is met: in simple terms, a safety net beneath an industry too important to lose. The need is real and urgent. Energy costs are among the highest in Europe, global overcapacity is squeezing prices, trade barriers are closing markets and the short-term cost of the transition to the low-carbon steel of the future weighs heavily on the industry of today.

 But I must also be honest with the Senedd: welcoming this Bill is a bittersweet experience. When the blast furnaces at Port Talbot hung in the balance in 2024, we were told by one Government in Westminster and then the next that nationalisation was not on the table. Roughly 2,600 people lost their livelihoods. A year later, when Scunthorpe''s furnaces came under the same threat, Parliament was recalled on a Saturday to keep them burning. Suddenly, every option was on the table. That was the difference, and Wales felt it keenly. I deeply regret that the purpose at the heart of this legislation before us today was not embraced in time to save the blast furnaces at Port Talbot. The belief is wide and deep in Wales that our steel industry was not treated equally. We do not ask for preferential treatment for Wales, but we do expect to be treated with the same fairness and the same dignity.

 Even so, yesterday''s regret must not stop us from protecting tomorrow. I welcome the fact that this tool will now be firmly in the toolbox for Wales, as for every other part of the United Kingdom. And let''s also be clear about what this Bill does not do. It does not guarantee the future of the Welsh steel industry. No statute can do that. What it guarantees is that, if the hour of danger comes again, Government can act within days rather than despair for months, so long as these powers remain on the statute book where they''re needed. We will oppose any attempt to let them quietly slip from it. A sunset clause on these powers contained in the Bill must never become the sunset of an industry.

 We also must be equally clear about something else: a safety net for one producer must not pull the floor from under the others. If British Steel in Scunthorpe is nationalised, a state-owned undertaking must not undermine the commercial position of 7 Steel or Tata Steel. A company relying on the public purse should not price below other producers, nor enjoy an unfair advantage in public procurement, nor draw investment and energy support away from Welsh sites. The Welsh Government will hold the UK Government to this principle at every stage.

 The Bill deals with both reserved and devolved matters, and it will affect all four nations of the United Kingdom. I told the UK Government clearly that the lack of time given to this Government and to the Senedd to scrutinise the Bill was unacceptable. Urgency is never an excuse for disregarding devolution. We did insist on clear commitments to protect Welsh interests and we secured important undertakings as a result, and I will return to them in closing.

 While there is currently no intention or need to use these powers in Wales, it is right that they exist, and it is the UK Government that holds the resources to act at this scale. Let''s remind ourselves what is at stake. Once the electric arc furnace at Port Talbot is operational—the second largest anywhere in the world—nearly half of the UK''s steel-making capacity will be here in Wales. The future of steel in these islands is a Welsh future. The Bill does not secure that future by itself, but it ensures that no Government can ever again claim that the means to defend it, if necessary, is missing. It''s on this basis, clear-eyed and with our expectations on the record, that I recommend the Senedd gives its consent.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Adam Price', 'Cabinet Minister for Enterprise, Connectivity and Energy', '5030', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=20149&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.220284', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767876', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ed0caaa4c76e1efb094b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Well, as I said earlier, the Government is accelerating the roll-out of 12.5 hours of funded childcare for all two-year-olds across Wales. The additional funding in the supplementary budget should enable local authorities to make progress on this first phase of the expansion. I am pleased that officials are having positive feedback from local authorities on the difference that the funding will make.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=3239&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.474763', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767561', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ed109e214efce399144e', 'legislation', 'Acts of Senedd Cymru', 'Planning (Wales) Act 2026', 'Planning (Wales) Act 2026', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/2026/6/2026-04-28/data.xml', '2026-07-07', '', '', '', '', '', 'legislation.gov.uk', '', '', '', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', '', 'Planning (Wales) Act', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:14.610728', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/data.feed', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ee13c234d6520277a7eb', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', 'Thank you for that question. The Member made an important point at the outset in talking about the importance of the supplementary budget yesterday, in providing £145 million in this financial year in order to address waiting lists, to improve infrastructure and to improve the provision that we have for waiting lists. It was incredibly disappointing to see Labour and Liberal Members siding with Reform to vote against policies and against funding that would tackle one of the most pressing issues that has faced us in health in Wales over a decade and more.

 But, we will continue with our plans to address those waiting lists, and this will directly benefit the people of Powys. We are aware of people who have been on waiting lists for far too long in Powys. They will benefit from this. But, we will also ensure that there is a rapid diagnostic centre for cancer introduced in Powys too so that people are seen earlier, rather than people going to hospitals that are long distances away when they get to stage 4 in their cancer journey, which will be far too late for many of them. We will see them more quickly and ensure that they get an earlier diagnosis too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=619&l=en', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', 'Bill progress', 'Stage 4; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.729565', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768025', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('eeda524f0c4cd39c4413', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I thought it was important for me to speak on this topic, given that, in my previous role as leader of Cardiff Council, one of the final decisions I took formally was the decision to introduce the visitor levy to the capital city.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=14074&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.985824', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767115', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f0bb653e79594206264b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much for the question and I was very pleased to see that this announcement was part of the announcement on the Government''s legislative programme. We are clearly committed to introducing a Bill on community right to buy and that will be a priority for us in the next few months now.

 I am very aware of a great number of community groups that are keen to buy and to procure buildings that become empty in their communities, in order to turn them into assets that are of use to the community, to bring life to the community and to the high street quite often as well. I am very aware as well that there are barriers quite often to doing this, and sometimes they lose the opportunity to buy an asset and to transform it for community use. So, I do hope that we all will be able to agree on new legislation that will empower community groups, so that they can identify, in the first place, assets that are in their community that they might want to buy for the future, that we create a register identifying all of those, and that then, when they do come on the market, they get first offer on those properties, and then have an opportunity to make a business case and to continue with the process. That is not happening at the moment and opportunities are being missed.

 I do intend to arrange a series of workshops over the summer now with partners, with communities, in order for us to have more detail in terms of this new legislation. I encourage you all to take part in that and encourage community groups in your constituencies to be part of this new important legislation.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3675&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'community right to buy; legislative programme; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.032842', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768143', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f29b133516adee0315fc', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '[ Inaudible .]—the idea that, from opposition, we were unable to resolve the problems of the M4—. We were in opposition over those years. He was in the Conservative Party, so I get the confusion there about where his politics land. Listen, I understand that there is frustration in wanting a holiday after eight weeks of being the leader of Reform in here. We will be working through the last 43 days of the 100-day plan, every single day of that reporting on it as we promised the people of Wales we would, and then cracking on with phase 2, which is that programme for government to transform the lives of the people of Wales. But today we need to get a supplementary budget through, to get working on the NHS, on childcare, on additional learning needs and all those issues that matter to the people of Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=1176&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.294052', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767498', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f3a307780b2f86029e34', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', 'Thank you very much, Llywydd. I''m very pleased to provide this update today on the work to develop the initial phasing and costs of the Government''s ambitious commitment to deliver a universal childcare offer.

 Members will be aware that the Government is introducing a phased expansion of funded universal childcare, which, at full roll-out, will be the most generous offer in the UK, providing 20 hours of funded childcare each week, for 48 weeks of the year, for all children aged from nine months to four years old.

 In my view, and the view of the Government, and in the view of every expert on poverty and child development, the expansion of childcare is not merely a ''nice to have'', but it''s absolutely essential. Whether or not to expand funded childcare is not a question to discuss. The lack of affordable childcare is a societal problem to solve, and in solving it, we will deliver benefits that span across gender equality, early child development, tackling poverty, supporting our economy, and improving our children''s health and educational outcomes.

 Having stated with pride our aim of delivering a universal childcare offer, and with us now 57 days into this Government term, we have progressed with this and made excellent progress. We have been working on our plans with local government and sector representatives through the newly established expert group, and we have already announced an additional £55 million of funding through the supplementary budget to support the initial phase of the expansion programme.

 As part of the process of developing our manifesto, we carefully costed an expanded childcare offer, and we identified the additional funding required to deliver the full expansion. And now in Government, officials have undertaken further economic modelling.

 On that basis, we are developing clearer costs, considering the assumptions behind them. The modelling undertaken so far suggests that, at today''s prices, the core costs of the full expansion of our offer will require an additional £375 million a year. This is a mid-point estimate, and work is ongoing with our economists and analysts to test and model a range of variables, which includes the costs at each phase of expansion and potential take-up rates.

 As advised by our expansion of childcare expert steering group, we will also take into account not only workforce costs, but a range of factors, including training and development, additional learning needs, local authority support and capital. The level of additional funding required, indicated by Welsh Government economists to date, aligns with the detailed work undertaken by Plaid Cymru in preparation to make this policy commitment.

 Our clear aim is that families of all children from nine months to four years across Wales should be able to access a more affordable, simpler, more consistent childcare offer by the end of this Senedd term. Achieving this aim will require careful sequencing, along with partnership working and ongoing funding decisions, but we are determined to maintain the momentum.

 The latest data, published on 1 July, shows that we are now offering funded childcare to nearly 75 per cent of all two-year-olds across Wales in 2025-26. However, we want to go further and ensure that what was promised by the previous Labour Government, but not delivered, is fully implemented. I want us to reach all our two-year-old children as quickly as possible and I am urging local authorities to complete this first phase by the end of this financial year. This is what families want and deserve.

 At the start of this Government term, three local authorities had succeeded in completing the roll-out of 12.5 hours of funded childcare to all two-year olds. One other local authority has reached every two-year-old child last month. And 16 more local authorities have already confirmed that, with the additional funding that we will invest through the supplementary budget, they''ll be able to move to a full roll-out by the end of this financial year, which demonstrates our commitment to deliver our new offer to families across Wales. Officials are working closely with the remaining two local authorities to understand how they can be supported to deliver this phase at this point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Sioned Williams', 'Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=11243&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.274923', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767777', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f3b9d62a95743f84542a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Thank you for your answer, Minister. You are responsible for your party''s programme for government, and that programme did take a significant blow last week. Just like the supplementary budget that you are hoping to pass, with votes you don''t have, your minority Government has to follow the first rule of politics, which is learning to count. There''s little doubt that much of Plaid''s manifesto hinged on an optimistic change of the funding model from Westminster. Now that the Senedd has rejected your calls to change the funding model, what changes have you made to your programme for government?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Cai Parry-Jones', '', '12174', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=4077&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.559240', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767605', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f51cdac1a40baec59a99', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'Forward work programme – Finance Committee', 'Forward work programme – Finance Committee

Forward work programme – Finance Committee Forward work programme – Finance Committee Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Finance Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. Business type: Other First published: 15/06/2026 Documents Forward Work Plan : Autumn 2026 PDF 59 KB Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47639', '2026-08-05', '', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 42.0, 'Low', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Finance Committee', 0, '2026-08-05T15:11:46.852090', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47639', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f61ae7fc42b305838bd1', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Diolch, Lywydd. I''m pleased to make my first contribution in this Chamber in my capacity as Chair of the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee. Financial scrutiny is an important function carried out by policy committees, alongside our work on policy and legislation. It is essential that we examine how public money is allocated and whether spending decisions support the delivery of Welsh Government priorities within our remit. The committee''s role is not simply to note budget changes, but to seek transparency and ensure accountability for the Welsh Government expenditure.

 We therefore decided at the committee''s first formal meeting on 8 July to consider the first supplementary budget as part of that work. We also noted the relevance of the Welsh Government''s 100-day priorities in areas within our remit. We are particularly interested in understanding how commitments related to nature recovery, flood resilience, coal-tip safety, the sustainable farming scheme, rural-proofing and the national food strategy will be funded and delivered in practice. These are substantial policy commitments and they raise important questions for financial scrutiny. Will they be delivered from existing budgets or will they require additional expenditure? As no additional money has been provided in the supplementary budget, are we going to have further allocations expected in the draft budget for 2027-28?

 We have also written to the Cabinet Minister for Finance seeking answers to these questions and clarification on a number of issues. For example, we would welcome further information on the Welsh Government''s energy service budget line and the decision to retain it within the rural resilience and sustainability main expenditure group. Despite responsibility for energy policy and strategy sitting elsewhere, it''s important that budget structures and ministerial responsibilities are clear and transparent.

 We have also raised why there are no major changes to the rural resilience and sustainability MEG, apart from the funding linked to Great British Energy, and whether the relevant Government department saw any additional resources during the supplementary budget process, and if not, why not? We further asked the Welsh Government to explain why there is no additional funding for biodiversity within the supplementary budget. Our predecessor committee raised concerns that Wales is currently not on track to meet the 30x30 target and recommended increases in allocations in this area in light of nature decline. Against that backdrop, the fact that there is no change to biodiversity allocations raises questions about whether existing funding is enough to deliver meaningful progress on whether actions will be needed to avoid Wales missing these targets.

 As Chair, and I know the committee will agree with me, we look forward to continuing the committee''s scrutiny of these issues and to considering the Government''s response to our questions. While the supplementary budget contains limited changes in the areas within the committee''s remit, it also provides an important opportunity to question how the Welsh Government''s commitments will be funded and delivered, and to ensure that resources are directed effectively towards climate, environmental sustainability and rural challenges facing Wales. Diolch yn fawr.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=25552&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; draft budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.761591', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767969', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f67a78edb78d5e5ea40d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Diolch. We''ve had many Members put in to speak in this debate, but, unfortunately, we will not be able to call everybody. We''re up against time.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26007&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.805538', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768006', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f6ed39a7f08dfeda81cd', 'calendar', 'Senedd forward look', 'Finance Committee — 01 October 2026, 09.30', 'Finance Committee is scheduled to meet on 01 October 2026 at 09.30.
Meeting status: Confirmed; Hybrid
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=984&MId=16294', '2026-10-01', '', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '', '16294', '', '2026-10-01', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-07T07:54:11.958265', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f741d1b877e45f35166d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Of course, this Bill does confer powers on the Secretary of State to make regulations to nationalise a steel undertaking in the United Kingdom. While it will not immediately nationalise any steel company, it does give the Secretary of State powers to take action in certain circumstances, which could include steel undertakings in Wales. These are significant powers, and whilst I understand the reasons they are being sought, I believe they should be approached with caution.

 Before the closure of Tata Steel''s blast furnace at Port Talbot, Wales''s steel accounted for 60 per cent of total UK crude steel output and primary iron, it employed over 4,000 people—a figure that has now sadly halved—it contributed around £3.2 billion per year to the Welsh economy, and it represented approximately 3 per cent of Wales''s total economic output. These figures demonstrate just how important the steel industry is to Wales, not only to our economy, but to thousands of families and communities that have depended on it for generations. Any decisions affecting the future of this sector will have lasting consequences.

 Of course, the Conservatives have always recognised the vital importance of our Port Talbot Tata Steel site and, to help secure its long-term future, it was, in fact, the UK Conservative Government that agreed a joint investment package with Tata Steel. Rather than taking the company into public ownership, it chose to work in partnership with the private sector to deliver a sustainable future for steel making in south Wales. That package included a £500 million Government grant to help fund a £1.25 billion transformation to a modern, greener electric arc furnace. This was a substantial commitment by the UK Conservative Government, demonstrating that Government can play an active role in supporting strategic industry whilst also encouraging private investment and innovation. Whilst it is important that Governments are able to step in and support our vital steel industry when circumstances demand it, we must never forget the benefits that come from Governments working constructively alongside the private sector. Partnership can deliver investment, safeguard jobs, modernise production, help secure the long-term future of our steel industry, and without making nationalisation the default option. Our focus should actually here remain on creating the conditions that encourage investment, protect skilled jobs, and ensure that Welsh steel continues to have a strong future.

 Whilst I do understand the intentions behind this Bill, my Welsh Conservative colleagues and I cannot and will not support this LCM.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Janet Finch-Saunders', '', '212', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=21393&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'LCM; Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.364377', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767880', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f82dcf119430a848005a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', 'Thank you very much to the Member for raising that important issue. As with the previous question, clearly, I am answering as Trefnydd today, but it is relevant in terms of the debate later today on the supplementary budget, because, clearly, there is £2 million allocated in that supplementary budget to start to address the challenges facing schools, and a percentage of that funding will be for travel costs.

 In addition to that, there is a Government commitment to carry out an analysis of all of the sports assets, including swimming pools, that we have across Wales to truly understand (1) what''s been lost and (2) what is at risk of being lost, because many of our swimming pools are very old, in order to put a long-term plan into place. Everyone should have access to swimming lessons. Everyone in Wales should have access to swimming pools. But, unfortunately, there have been cuts, and that''s why this work must proceed. I would encourage any Member here today who is passionate, as am I and as is my party, about ensuring that every child in primary school in Wales has a right to access swimming lessons free of charge, in order to learn such an important skill, to support the supplementary budget today.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=6562&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.800071', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767718', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f895e1cd7a161d77af6e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'How on earth, as a party that talks about consensus, mature politics and compromise, has it managed to unite most of this Chamber against it? It doesn''t bode well for the future. It''s going to be a very long Senedd if this is how Plaid Cymru do Government. This supplementary budget could have been an opportunity to right the wrongs of the past Labour Government, to safeguard jobs for student nurses and student midwives. It could have been an opportunity to offer ALN a lot more funding than Plaid Cymru is offering right now. On page 39 of Plaid''s manifesto, it says that

 ''A Plaid Cymru government will address key challenges in the Additional Learning Needs (ALN) system in Wales''.

 But today, Plaid are asking us to vote for a budget that rejects an opportunity to give a huge boost to ALN children, and that is what this Chamber is asking for. It''s about time that ALN and education had a significant boost given the educational outcomes in Wales. They are way behind the rest of the UK. Education, quite frankly, has always been a poor relation to the NHS, the budget of which now takes up half the Welsh Government''s expenditure, so this would''ve been an opportunity to rebalance the funding across services in the two main spending areas in Wales. But the truth is that Plaid are using a windfall of consequential funding to plug a financial black hole hidden in its manifesto. It''s being used to help fund the first phases of implementation, and without this windfall, Plaid would''ve been lost.

 We aren''t expecting the entire Government budget to be rewritten, although it is not unusual for new UK Governments—indeed, Governments across the world—to issue wide-ranging budgets a few months after they''ve been elected and taken office. But to limit this supplementary budget to the lines table demonstrates a very low appetite to do things differently. Plaid will say that the allocation of moneys reflects their manifesto priorities, but funding their commitments largely from a one-off windfall is not sustainable, and there is a significant lack of detail as to how that funding will make a real impact on the day-to-day lives of the Welsh people. How will it make a difference? What improvements will we see and when?

 Llywydd, it is not just the budget numbers that don''t add up; the votes are not adding up for Plaid either this evening. The Tories and Labour have said they''ll oppose this budget, so assuming that there''s not a secret deal struck at the eleventh hour, this budget will fall. And it will fall because Plaid are being incredibly stubborn over an amount of money that its First Minister has previously labelled ''small change''. Reform is willing to offer Plaid Cymru a lifeline, and it will only cost you some small change, as you call it—the small change needed to guarantee student nurses a job; some small change taken back from overseas ventures and ploughed back into Welsh public services; and some serious small change to make a huge investment into ALN, which is badly needed. And after all, that is why we have this consequential funding coming to Wales.

 If Plaid can commit to those commonsense priorities, priorities that have a huge amount of support across Wales, I''m sure that we can go into recess on a high note. But if you lose this vote, you will have tripped at the very first, very low hurdle. We will be heading into the summer questioning whether this Government has the ability and the support to continue leading Wales. Plaid will need to have a long, hard think over the summer and come back willing to compromise. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Dan Thomas', '', '12147', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24832&l=en', NULL, 66.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.670648', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767905', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('f8f50a885d5b6f43e27c', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'I warmly welcome the Welsh Government''s commitment to a national home retrofitting programme. Good housing is good health. Better-insulated homes will not only reduce energy bills and carbon emissions, they also improve people''s health and reduce pressure on the NHS. Public Health Wales estimates that tackling excess cold in Welsh homes alone can save the NHS more than £40 million a year. Living in cold, damp and mouldy homes also increases the risk of respiratory illnesses, particularly in children and older people. So, Cabinet Minister, can you outline how the Welsh Government''s retrofitting programme will prioritise tackling damp, mould and excess cold in homes so that it improves public health, reduces pressure on the NHS and lowers energy bills for families across Wales?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Safa Elhassan', '', '12189', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5808&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; energy_efficiency', 'Private rented sector; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'damp; mould; retrofitting', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.257206', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768191', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('fcf26d9e7191ec5f67d7', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '3. Topical Questions', 'Thank you very much for bringing this issue forward before the Senedd. One thinks about the people who were affected by this fire. I can only imagine how frightened they must have been during that period when the flames were roaring on the mountain in Penmaenmawr, an area I''ve very familiar with. Thirty-six households had to flee their homes during the work of putting out the fire, but luckily they could return home on Monday evening. Conwy council provided temporary accommodation for those who needed it, so we are very grateful to Conwy council for their work and very grateful to Natural Resources Wales, and, of course, to the fire service. Fighting wildfires is very difficult and dangerous work, and we need to pay tribute to all the firefighters in north Wales and the partner agencies who responded so quickly to this event and kept communities and people safe.

 You asked what support the Welsh Government can provide. Well, of course, we are working with local partners. The county council is in all likelihood the first port of call in terms of understanding the problems that may continue now because of what''s happened, and also NRW in terms of restoring the land and any work that needs to happen on the nature side of things. But you''re right, this is shocking and makes us realise the severity of the situation in terms of the climate, the nature emergency and climate emergency that we''re facing here. I don''t think anybody could start to deny that that''s very real. Between these fires and this incredible heat that we''ve had, which is also affecting people in very negative ways, we genuinely have to tackle this crisis facing us in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Topical Questions', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=6284&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister', '', 'temporary accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.302236', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768218', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('fd3bf23e7d09530ce405', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much. I do understand the point about having annual targets, but often work can happen towards the end of the period. So, setting rigid annual targets, therefore, the same target every year, wouldn''t reflect the reality of the situation, because it doesn''t happen in a linear way. Often, the process starts and it accelerates towards the end of the period. But I will look to see how transparent—. I want to be transparent, and I want to provide you with as much information as possible. I''m going to be asking to have an operational role in some of the meetings that are happening with civil servants for me to understand exactly what''s going on.

 And in terms of the housing associations bringing old stock up to standard, there is another grant available for that, the optimised retrofit scheme, which is very successful in terms of improving the condition of our social housing. We need to accelerate that, and we also need to see the private sector stock progressing as well, so that the quality of our housing everywhere and across all tenacies improves.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5677&l=en', NULL, 88.0, 'High', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency; social_housing', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'Optimised Retrofit; housing associations; retrofit; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.240470', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768222', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('fe12b36a9262ca6e2eb4', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'First Supplementary Budget 2026-27

First Supplementary Budget 2026-27 First Supplementary Budget 2026-27 Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings Related Issues The Welsh Government laid its First Supplementary Budget 2026-27 on 23 June 2026. The budget amends the Welsh Government Final Budget for 2026-27. The Finance Committee considered the First Supplementary Budget 2026-27 at its meeting on 30 June 2026 . The Finance Committee published its report (PDF,418KB) Scrutiny of the First Supplementary Budget 2026-27 on 13 July 2026. Business type: Budget Scrutiny Reason considered: Senedd Business; Status: For consideration First published: 24/06/2026 Documents Letter from Rhondda Cynon Taf Council: Support for Additional Learning Needs funding - 13 July 2026 PDF 208 KB Letter from the Chair of the Early Years, Children, Young People and Education Committee to the Cabinet Minister for Finance - 13 July 2026 PDF 132 KB Letter from the Chair of the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee to the Cabinet Minister for Finance in relation to the Welsh Government First Supplementary Budget 2026-27- 8 July 2026 PDF 107 KB Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47698', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 91.0, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; Finance Committee; final budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:46.404354', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47698', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('fed22483c121969306f8', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'Thank you very much for those comments. On the timing of this statement, I would make it clear, Llywydd, that we are following in both spirit and in practice the precise protocols that have always been followed and were followed by the previous Government, and this is the statement that I''m making in the Senedd Chamber this afternoon.

 I welcome the Member taking an opportunity to speak about a number of the legislative opportunities that he would like to pursue. Commenting on the ones in our programme that he referred to, I welcome his support for the principle of introducing the rural-proofing legislation. There''s a lot of work to be done around even defining what ''rural'' means, because currently, in statutory terms, we don''t have that. So, it''s a matter of ensuring that we have the frameworks within which, then, we can say that, in everything Government does, it has to take into account the impact of decisions on rural Wales. It''s very, very important.

 Also on protecting renters, I welcome that support. This is building, indeed, on current legislation, and bridging to future legislation. There is further legislation that this Government would wish to introduce to protect renters further, but I think it''s important that we keep an eye on how we can build consensus on the need to protect renters in this key sector.

 On the statutory school support staff legislation, you''re right, this has been a topic of conversation, debate and vote in this Senedd recently, and as was implied in the way the vote went, I can see that benefits of a statutory body that would give support staff similar arrangements to those set up in England, giving them a similar status to our teachers, who already have an independent pay review body. So, I''m very keen, as was said during that particular debate, to investigate how that could be taken forward. I would say, though, as I referred to in the statement itself, we legislate where we need to legislate, and we always look if there are other ways in which we can get the results we want. So, this Government is determined to make an immediate difference to people''s lives, and I''m keen to explore what we can do without legislation too, and I would invite those who would work with us on those aspects of that particular potential legislation.

 On community right to buy, again, I welcome the support in principle, and, yes, Wales has slipped behind here. I appreciate that work has been done in previous Governments, but those Governments didn''t get it over the line. So, we have an opportunity now to do that. We believe in our communities, we believe in empowering communities, and if we''re able to work on this, because working together can bring results, we will drive this forward in the interest of communities the length and breadth of Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=9602&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community right to buy; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.082784', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767747', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('ff80e662ab76cea79c67', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Diolch, Lywydd. I''m grateful to the Cabinet Minister for bringing this motion forward. Let me begin with a word about the scale of the task. This is one of seven legislative consent memoranda the Legislation Committee has considered, and we have been operational for just three weeks. At least two more are waiting for us in September. If I may say so, we really have hit the ground running, and I''d like to thank everyone in the committee who has worked so hard with me to do this. At our very first meeting, we recognised that scrutinising consent memoranda would be core to our work, and seven in three weeks rather proves the point. But that, of course, is the work of the committee, and it is work we are glad to do. I want to raise three matters with the Senedd today: the timing of this memorandum, the absence of a recommendation and the substance of what the committee found. 

 First, timing. The memorandum was not laid until six weeks after the Bill''s introduction to the House of Commons. We acknowledge that the Bill arrived very soon after this Welsh Government was formed, and we do recognise that was challenging, but the Welsh Government would have known that the UK Parliament had agreed to expedite the Bill through its stages, so the pressure on scrutiny time was foreseeable. Standing Orders expect a consent memorandum to be laid, normally, within two weeks of a Bill''s introduction. Better observance of that expectation would greatly improve the Senedd''s opportunity to scrutinise, and we have written to the First Minister to say so.

 Second, the recommendation, or rather its absence. The first eight consent memoranda laid before this Senedd contained no recommendation on whether the Senedd should consent. Instead, in each case, the responsible Minister says a recommendation will follow once the Welsh Government has analysed the wider impact of the UK legislative programme for Wales. We are genuinely unclear why so broad an analysis should bear on recommendation about an individual Bill, and we have written to the First Minister seeking clarity.

 On this Bill, a recommendation to consent did reach us, but not until a letter on 7 July, by which point we already had to consider this memorandum on 6 July in order to meet the timescales for today''s debate. That timetable also forced a decision I want to be transparent about. Because our time was so limited, we wrote directly to the Secretary of State for Business and Trade, supporting the Cabinet Minister''s request to strengthen the Welsh Government''s role in the exercise of any future powers should a steel undertaking in Wales be nationalised. Let me be clear about why I flag this. We did so because of the specific compressed circumstances of this Bill, and it should not be read as the committee''s settled preference. Our preferred position always is the fullest possible protection for the role of the Senedd as the legislature responsible for devolved matters in Wales.

 Third, the substance. The Cabinet Minister''s 7 July letter updated us on inter-governmental negotiations. We now know the UK Government has given a despatch box commitment that it will ordinarily consult the Welsh Ministers if the transfer powers are likely to be used in relation to a steel undertaking whose principal place of business is in Wales. We also note that the Welsh Government used the Interministerial Standing Committee to press the importance of parliamentary timetables that give this Senedd and its committees the maximum opportunity to scrutinise.

 Llywydd, the committee offers a number of conclusions. First, we agree with the Welsh Government''s assessment of the provisions in the Bill that require the Senedd''s consent. Second, the Welsh Government advocating for the Senedd to have maximum opportunity to consider Bills that trigger the consent process is to be welcomed. Third, poor communications between the two Governments is regrettable and has seemingly contributed to the delay in laying this memorandum and so the time available for scrutiny. Fourth, the absence of the Welsh Government''s recommendation on consent at the point we had to scrutinise the memorandum plainly affected our scrutiny, and we hope that Senedd committees will not regularly find themselves in that position in future. Fifth, as we said in our letter to the First Minister on 8 July, we remain unclear why the impact of the UK''s Government legislative programme as a whole should influence a Welsh Minister''s recommendation on an individual Bill. We do not consider this a desirable approach, and we hope it will not be repeated when future UK legislative programmes are announced. And, on that despatch-box commitment, it is not binding on any Government, its value weakened further by the fact it reflects only the current UK''s Government''s intentions, not the action a future Government could lawfully take.

 Finally, I would draw the Senedd''s attention to a document laid only recently: the Counsel General''s written statement, ''Welsh Government Principles on UK Legislation in Devolved Areas''. Those principles state, and I quote, that:

 ''non-legislative intergovernmental agreements, such as Memoranda of Understanding and ministerial assurances, should not be relied upon in place of statutory provision''.

 This is the Government''s stated principle, yet, on the Bill, it has accepted precisely such an assurance in place of statutory provision. The role of the Senedd should not depend on goodwill alone. Safeguards that can be withdrawn are no safeguards at all.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Sarah Cooper-Lesadd', '', '12167', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=20480&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Legislation Committee; Legislative Consent Motion; legislative programme', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.261526', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767889', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
